var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/variation/#Variation","page":"Variation","text":"A variation operator makes a child from one or two parents. The operators below work on vectors of real numbers; a genotype of another type gets a vary method for an operator of its own, as in the graph tutorial.","title":"Variation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/variation/#Interface","page":"Variation","text":"","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/variation/#Operators","page":"Variation","text":"","title":"Operators"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.AbstractVariation","page":"Variation","text":"AbstractVariation\n\nSupertype of variation operators, which turn one or two parent genotypes into a child.\n\nRequired methods\n\nA mutation defines vary(rng, op, parent); a crossover defines vary(rng, op, p1, p2) and n_parents(op) returning 2.\n\nA user with a custom genotype defines a new operator type and a vary method for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractVariation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.vary","page":"Variation","text":"vary(rng::AbstractRNG, op::AbstractVariation, parent)\nvary(rng::AbstractRNG, op::AbstractVariation, p1, p2)\n\nOne child of parent, or of the parents p1 and p2. Does not modify its parents, and returns a new object, not one of them: the parents are the genotypes the archive stores, and the child becomes one if the archive takes it. An operator that changed its parent in place would change an elite, which then no longer belongs to its cell. test_variation checks this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.vary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.n_parents","page":"Variation","text":"n_parents(op::AbstractVariation) -> Int\n\nNumber of parents op combines: 1 for a mutation, which is the default, and 2 for a crossover. VariationEmitter draws that many parents from the archive for each child. The value must be known from the type of op, so that the choice is made at compile time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.n_parents"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.GaussianMutation","page":"Variation","text":"GaussianMutation(σ)\n\nMutation that adds independent Normal(0, σ) noise to every entry of a real-valued vector. σ must be positive and finite.\n\nvary(rng, op, parent) returns a new array with the indices of parent, made with similar (a Vector for a Vector), whose entries have the floating-point type of those of parent: Float32 for a Vector{Float32}, Float64 for an integer vector. σ is converted to that type. parent is left unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GaussianMutation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.IsoLineVariation","page":"Variation","text":"IsoLineVariation(σ_iso, σ_line)\nIsoLineVariation(; σ_iso=0.01, σ_line=0.2)\n\nCrossover for real-valued vectors that moves along the line through two parents (Vassiliades and Mouret, 2018). For parents p1 and p2 the child is\n\np1 + σ_iso * z + σ_line * u * (p2 - p1)\n\nwith z a vector of independent standard normal numbers and u one standard normal number. The first noise term spreads the child around p1 in every direction (isotropic), the second stretches it along the line to p2. Both standard deviations must be non-negative and finite; σ_line = 0 leaves a Gaussian mutation of p1, and both zero a copy of p1.\n\nThe two parents must have the same indices. vary(rng, op, p1, p2) returns a new array with those indices, made with similar from p1 (a Vector for a Vector), whose entries have the floating-point type that the entries of the two parents promote to: Float32 for two Vector{Float32}s. The standard deviations are converted to that type. The parents are left unchanged.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20), variation=IsoLineVariation(0.01, 0.2))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.IsoLineVariation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.PolynomialMutation","page":"Variation","text":"PolynomialMutation(lower, upper; eta=20.0, proportion=1.0)\n\nMutation for real-valued vectors in a box, the bounded polynomial mutation of Deb and Goyal (1996) used in NSGA-II. Each entry is changed with probability proportion. A changed entry moves by a random step whose density falls off polynomially with the distance, and the step is drawn so that the result never leaves [lower, upper]: near a bound the steps shrink on that side, and an entry exactly on a bound only moves inward. Larger eta gives smaller steps; the usual values are 5 to 50. A step has size about (upper - lower) / (eta + 1).\n\nlower and upper are numbers, the same for every entry, or vectors with one bound per entry. Both must be finite and lower < upper. eta must be non-negative and proportion between 0 and 1; the textbook value of proportion is 1 / n for vectors of length n.\n\nvary(rng, op, parent) requires every entry of parent to lie inside the bounds, and throws an ArgumentError otherwise; an archive fed by other operators or by a random_solution that ignore the bounds can hold such a parent, so give every emitter of a run the same bounds. It returns a new array with the indices of parent, made with similar (a Vector for a Vector), with the element type of parent (Float64 for integers), and leaves parent unchanged.\n\nChanging an entry takes two floating-point powers, about 50 ns, so a child of 20 entries costs around 1 µs where a GaussianMutation takes under 100 ns; a proportion below 1 reduces it in proportion.\n\nThe mutation in QDax (polynomial_mutation) differs from the original in the power term, δ where the original has 1 - δ. There, an entry on a bound never moves, and one near it is often clipped onto it. This implementation follows the original, as pymoo does.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> op = PolynomialMutation(-5.12, 5.12; eta=20);\n\njulia> child = QualityDiversity.vary(Xoshiro(1), op, [5.12, 0.0, -5.12]);\n\njulia> all(-5.12 .<= child .<= 5.12)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.PolynomialMutation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/variation/#QualityDiversity.UniformCrossover","page":"Variation","text":"UniformCrossover(; proportion=0.5)\n\nCrossover for genotypes that are vectors of any element type. Each entry of the child is taken from the second parent with probability proportion, and from the first parent otherwise. With proportion = 0 the child copies the first parent and with 1 the second. The parents must have the same indices.\n\nIt works for real numbers, integers, symbols or any other entries, since it only copies them. It does not create values that neither parent has; combine it with a mutation for that, for example in two emitters.\n\nvary(rng, op, p1, p2) returns a new array with the indices of the parents, made with similar from p1 (a Vector for a Vector), whose element type holds the entries of both parents, and leaves the parents unchanged.\n\nQDax calls its version of this operator polynomial_crossover; it copies a random subset of entries, drawn with replacement, from the second parent.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> child = QualityDiversity.vary(Xoshiro(1), UniformCrossover(), [1, 2, 3, 4], [5, 6, 7, 8]);\n\njulia> all(c -> c in (1, 5) || c in (2, 6) || c in (3, 7) || c in (4, 8), child)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.UniformCrossover"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Emitters","page":"Emitters","text":"An emitter proposes a batch of candidates each round and may learn from what the archive did with them.","title":"Emitters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Interface","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Variation-emitter","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Variation emitter"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#CMA-emitter","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"CMA emitter"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Evolution-strategies","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Evolution strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Rankers","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Rankers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Selection-rules","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Selection rules"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Restart-rules","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Restart rules"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Gradient-emitters","page":"Emitters","text":"Emitters for differentiable quality diversity, which step along the gradients of the fitness and the descriptor; see evaluate_gradient. An emitter that needs the gradients at points of its own asks for them before every round.","title":"Gradient emitters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitters/#Bandit-scheduler","page":"Emitters","text":"","title":"Bandit scheduler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.AbstractEmitter","page":"Emitters","text":"AbstractEmitter\n\nSupertype of emitters, which propose batches of candidate solutions and may learn from how the archive received them.\n\nRequired methods\n\nask(rng, emitter, archive)\nbatch_size(emitter)\n\nOptional methods\n\ntell!(emitter, xs, evs, results)\nrestarts(emitter)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractEmitter"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ask","page":"Emitters","text":"ask(rng::AbstractRNG, emitter::AbstractEmitter, archive::AbstractArchive) -> Vector{G}\n\nA batch of batch_size(emitter) candidate genotypes. Required. Every candidate is a new object: the archive stores what it accepts by reference, so an emitter must not hand over a genotype it got from the archive or a buffer it writes to again. test_emitter checks this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.tell!","page":"Emitters","text":"tell!(emitter::AbstractEmitter, xs, evs, results)\n\nReport the candidates xs returned by ask, their evaluations evs, and the AddResults from the archive, so the emitter can update its internal state. Optional: the default does nothing, so an emitter without state needs no method. An emitter that keeps state about the batch it asked for may require that tell! follows that ask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.tell!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.batch_size","page":"Emitters","text":"batch_size(emitter::AbstractEmitter)\n\nNumber of candidates ask returns per call. Required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.batch_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.restarts","page":"Emitters","text":"restarts(emitter::AbstractEmitter) -> Union{Int,Nothing}\n\nHow many times emitter has restarted its search, or nothing for an emitter that never restarts. Optional: the default is nothing. A BanditScheduler with ReselectTerminated reads it after every round.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.restarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.VariationEmitter","page":"Emitters","text":"VariationEmitter(op; batch_size)\n\nEmitter that draws parents uniformly from the archive and applies the variation operator op, giving batch_size children. A mutation takes one parent per child and a crossover two, as n_parents(op) says; the parents are drawn with replacement. It keeps no state, so it is the emitter of MAP-Elites.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.VariationEmitter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.CMAEmitter","page":"Emitters","text":"CMAEmitter(dim; sigma0, batch_size, strategy, ranker, selection, restart, x0, lower, upper)\nCMAEmitter(x0::AbstractVector; sigma0, ...)\n\nEmitter that searches for solutions with CMA-ES, the covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy, in the sense of CMA-ME (Fontaine et al., 2020): it keeps a Gaussian distribution of solutions, and after every batch it moves the distribution toward the candidates that its ranker places first. The optimizer can be another evolution strategy. Solutions are real vectors of length dim; the genotype type of the archive can be any AbstractVector type that a vector of Float64 converts to.\n\nsigma0: the initial step size, the standard deviation of the distribution. It has no default, because it depends on the scale of the solutions.\nbatch_size: the number of candidates per batch, 4 + floor(3 log(dim)) by default (rounded up to an even number for OpenAIES).\nstrategy: the optimizer, a subtype of AbstractEvolutionStrategy or a function with the same arguments as its constructors. It is CMAES by default; SepCMAES and LMMAES scale to a high dimension (LMMAES only works there: it needs at least twice as many parameters as batch_size), and OpenAIES follows a gradient estimate. For an option of a strategy pass a function, such as (dim; kwargs...) -> LMMAES(dim; kwargs..., n_vectors=8).\nranker: how the candidates are ordered, TwoStageImprovementRanker by default. Use ImprovementRanker with a ThresholdArchive, ObjectiveRanker for an emitter that only maximizes the fitness, and TwoStageRandomDirectionRanker for one that explores a direction of descriptor space. The emitter keeps its own copy of the ranker.\nselection: how many of the ranked candidates are recombined, FilterSelection by default or MuSelection.\nrestart: when to start again, NoImprovementRestart by default.\nx0: the mean of the first distribution. Without it, and after every restart, the mean is a random elite of the archive, so the archive must hold a solution when the emitter first asks. The second form takes dim and x0 from a vector.\nlower, upper: bounds of the solutions, scalars or vectors. A sample outside is drawn again, and moved inside the bounds after 1000 draws. x0 must lie inside them.\n\nask draws a batch, and the next call of tell! must be with the results for that batch; a second ask before that is an error. tell! ranks the candidates, adapts the distribution and decides on a restart; the restart itself happens at the following ask, which has the random number generator and the archive to choose the new start from. The emitter has the properties iterations, the number of batches told, and restarts.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks, Random\n\njulia> prob = Rastrigin(10);\n\njulia> alg = CMAME(prob; dims=(20, 20), sigma0=1.0, num_emitters=3);\n\njulia> result = solve(prob, alg; budget=3_000, rng=Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> coverage(result.archive) > 0.1\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAEmitter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.AbstractEvolutionStrategy","page":"Emitters","text":"AbstractEvolutionStrategy\n\nSupertype of the optimizers inside a CMAEmitter: CMAES, SepCMAES, LMMAES and OpenAIES. A strategy keeps a search distribution over real vectors, draws batches from it, and moves it toward the solutions that the emitter's ranker places first. It maximizes: the ranking passed to update! lists the best solution first.\n\nA strategy has the fields dim, the length of a solution, batch_size, x, a dim × batch_size matrix that holds the last batch, one solution per column, and lower and upper, the bounds of a solution as vectors of length dim, which a CMAEmitter reads to move the elite that a search starts from inside them. It defines\n\nStrategyType(dim; sigma0, batch_size, lower, upper): sigma0 is the initial step size, lower and upper are the bounds of a solution, scalars or vectors, and batch_size has a default;\nreset!(es, x0): restart the search at x0;\nsample!(rng, es): draw a batch into es.x and return it, drawing a solution again if it falls outside the bounds. After 1000 draws outside, the last one moves to the nearest point inside the bounds: with the mean on the boundary of the box, a draw lands inside with a probability that halves with every dimension. A strategy that moves its mean along the stored steps of its solutions computes the step of a moved solution from where it lies;\nupdate!(es, order, parents): adapt to the last batch, where order ranks its solutions from best to worst and the first parents of them are recombined;\nshould_stop(es, keys): whether the search has converged or broken down, given the ranking keys of the last batch from best to worst; see rank_key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractEvolutionStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.CMAES","page":"Emitters","text":"CMAES(dim; sigma0, batch_size=default, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf, variant=TextbookCMA())\n\nCovariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy (Hansen and Ostermeier, 2001; Hansen, 2016, \"The CMA Evolution Strategy: A Tutorial\"), maximizing. It keeps a Gaussian search distribution with mean m, step size σ and covariance C, and is the default optimizer inside a CMAEmitter. variant, a CMAVariant, chooses the update: the tutorial's, or pyribs's through PyribsCMAES.\n\nA generation has two calls. sample!(rng, es) draws batch_size solutions, x = m + σ B D z with z standard normal and C = B D² Bᵀ, and leaves them in es.x as the columns of a matrix. After the caller has ranked them, update!(es, order, parents) moves the distribution toward the parents best solutions, the first entries of order. The number of parents may differ from one generation to the next, and none is allowed; the recombination weights log(parents + 1/2) - log(i) are normalized each time.\n\nbatch_size defaults to 4 + floor(3 log(dim)). With lower and upper, scalars or vectors, a sample is drawn again until it lies inside; after 1000 draws it moves to the nearest point inside the bounds, and the update uses the step to that point.\n\nshould_stop(es, keys) reports the termination criteria of CMA-ES: a covariance matrix with a condition number above 1e14, a distribution that has shrunk below 1e-11, a non-finite state, or keys, the ranking keys from best to worst, being flat: the best and the worst are within 1e-12 of each other. reset!(es, x0) restarts the search at x0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.PyribsCMAES","page":"Emitters","text":"PyribsCMAES(dim; sigma0, batch_size=default, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf)\n\nCMAES with the update of pyribs 0.12.0, PyribsCMA: given the same batches and rankings, its state equals that of pyribs's CMAEvolutionStrategy up to rounding. It exists to reproduce results that were produced with pyribs, such as those of the CMA-MAE paper, where its weak rank-one term makes a search explore with rounder distributions. Pass it as strategy=QualityDiversity.PyribsCMAES to a CMAEmitter, CMAME or CMAMAE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.PyribsCMAES"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.SepCMAES","page":"Emitters","text":"SepCMAES(dim; sigma0, batch_size=default, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf)\n\nSeparable CMA-ES (Ros and Hansen, 2008, \"A Simple Modification in CMA-ES Achieving Linear Time and Space Complexity\"), maximizing. It is CMAES with a diagonal covariance matrix: it adapts one variance for each coordinate and no correlations, which costs O(dim) for a generation instead of O(dim²) to O(dim³), and it learns faster in high dimension where there are too few samples to learn a full matrix. The learning rates of the covariance update are those of the reference implementation (pycma), which are larger than those of CMAES because there are only dim variances to learn. The step size is adapted as in CMAES.\n\nThe arguments, sample!, update! and should_stop are those of AbstractEvolutionStrategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.SepCMAES"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.LMMAES","page":"Emitters","text":"LMMAES(dim; sigma0, batch_size=default, n_vectors=batch_size, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf)\n\nLimited-memory matrix adaptation evolution strategy (Loshchilov, Glasmachers and Beyer, 2019, \"Large Scale Black-Box Optimization by Limited-Memory Matrix Adaptation\"), maximizing. For solutions of high dimension it replaces the covariance matrix by n_vectors vectors, which cost O(n_vectors * dim) memory and time for a solution, and it needs no eigendecomposition.\n\nA solution is x = m + σ d, where d is a standard normal vector z that the vectors M_j stretch one after the other, d ← (1 - c_d,j) d + c_d,j M_j (M_jᵀ d). After a generation the vectors and the path of the step size move toward the recombined z of the parents, and the step size follows the cumulative step size rule. The learning rates are c_σ = 2 batch_size / dim, c_d,j = 1 / (1.5^(j-1) dim) and c_c,j = batch_size / (4^(j-1) dim), as in pyribs; n_vectors is at most dim. The solutions of a batch are not mirrored.\n\nA high dimension only\n\nLM-MA-ES is meant for hundreds of parameters or more, where the covariance matrix of CMAES costs too much, and it requires a batch of at most half the dimension, 2 batch_size ≤ dim. Each generation the path of the step size keeps a fraction 1 - c_σ of itself; with a larger batch c_σ exceeds 1, the fraction is negative, the path changes sign every generation and the step size follows noise instead of the search. The constructor therefore throws for such a batch. With the default batch, 4 + floor(3 log(dim)), this means dim ≥ 26. For fewer parameters use CMAES or SepCMAES, or pass a smaller batch_size. pyribs refuses only batch_size > dim.\n\nThe arguments, sample!, update! and should_stop are those of AbstractEvolutionStrategy. should_stop also reports a step size below 1e-12.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.LMMAES"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.OpenAIES","page":"Emitters","text":"OpenAIES(dim; sigma0, batch_size=default, learning_rate=0.001, beta1=0.9,\n         beta2=0.999, epsilon=1e-8, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf)\n\nNatural evolution strategy in the form of Salimans et al. (2017, \"Evolution Strategies as a Scalable Alternative to Reinforcement Learning\"), maximizing. The search distribution is an isotropic Gaussian of fixed standard deviation sigma0 around a point θ. A batch consists of batch_size / 2 perturbations of θ and their mirror images, θ ± σ₀ ε. Each solution gets its centered rank r in [-1/2, 1/2], the gradient estimate is Σ ε (r₊ - r₋) / (batch_size/2 σ₀), and the Adam optimizer (Kingma and Ba, 2015) with the given hyperparameters moves θ along it.\n\nbatch_size is even, and by default the default of CMAES rounded up to an even number. With bounds, a pair of mirrored solutions is drawn again if either lies outside, and moved inside the bounds after 1000 draws, and θ is moved inside the bounds after every step, so that the pairs keep landing inside. Unlike for the covariance matrix strategies, the number of parents of update! has no effect: the ranks of the whole batch make up the gradient. A restart resets θ and the state of Adam. should_stop also reports a last step of θ below 1e-9 times its norm.\n\nThe arguments, sample!, update! and should_stop are those of AbstractEvolutionStrategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.OpenAIES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.reset!","page":"Emitters","text":"reset!(es::CMAES, x0)\n\nRestart the search at x0: the mean becomes x0, the step size sigma0, and the covariance matrix the identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.reset!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.sample!","page":"Emitters","text":"sample!(rng, es::CMAES)\n\nDraw a batch from the search distribution into es.x, one solution per column, and return es.x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.sample!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.update!","page":"Emitters","text":"update!(es::CMAES, order, parents)\n\nAdapt the distribution to the last batch. order ranks the solutions of the batch from best to worst, and the first parents of them are recombined. With no parent only the count of evaluations advances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.update!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.should_stop","page":"Emitters","text":"should_stop(es::CMAES, keys) -> Bool\n\nWhether the search has converged or broken down; see CMAES. keys are the ranking keys of the last batch from best to worst.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.should_stop"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.CMAVariant","page":"Emitters","text":"CMAVariant\n\nSupertype of the variants of the update of CMAES: TextbookCMA, the default, and PyribsCMA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAVariant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.TextbookCMA","page":"Emitters","text":"TextbookCMA()\n\nThe update of Hansen (2016), \"The CMA Evolution Strategy: A Tutorial\". This is the default of CMAES.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.TextbookCMA"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.PyribsCMA","page":"Emitters","text":"PyribsCMA()\n\nThe update that pyribs 0.12.0's CMAEvolutionStrategy computes, which departs from the tutorial in five places:\n\nThe rank-one term pc pcᵀ has weight c1²: pyribs multiplies it by c1 twice. The term is then much weaker, so a search stays rounder for longer.\nThe evolution path pc accumulates the mean shift m′ - m itself, not divided by σ, so the rank-one term scales with σ²: it vanishes for a small step size and dominates the covariance for a large one.\nThe step size changes by exp(min(1, c_σ/d_σ (‖p_σ‖²/n - 1)/2)), not exp(min(1, c_σ/d_σ (‖p_σ‖/χₙ - 1))).\nThe path pc stalls unless ‖p_σ‖²/n / (1 - (1 - c_σ)^{2g}) < 2 + 4/(n + 1), with g the generation, instead of ‖p_σ‖ / √(1 - (1 - c_σ)^{2g}) / χₙ < 1.4 + 2/(n + 1).\nThe eigendecomposition is recomputed when more than λ / (2 n (c1 + c_μ)) evaluations have passed, with c1 and c_μ those of λ ÷ 2 parents, also after a generation without parents; the matrix and its inverse square root are made symmetric by the larger of each pair of entries, and the eigenvalues are taken in absolute value.\n\nUse it through PyribsCMAES to reproduce results produced with pyribs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.PyribsCMA"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.AbstractRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"AbstractRanker\n\nSupertype of the rules that order the candidates of a CMAEmitter from best to worst after they have been evaluated and added to the archive. The optimizer moves toward the best ones, so the ranker decides what the emitter looks for.\n\nA ranker defines rank_key(ranker, ev, result), which gives a key for one candidate from its Evaluation and its AddResult. Candidates are ordered by descending key; ties keep their order in the batch. A key is a Float64 or a tuple of numbers that compares lexicographically. A batch is flat, and the search restarts, when the best and the worst key are the same.\n\nA ranker with state, such as a random direction, defines reset_ranker!, which an emitter calls whenever a search starts. An emitter keeps its own copy of its ranker.\n\nThe rankers are TwoStageImprovementRanker, ImprovementRanker, ObjectiveRanker, TwoStageObjectiveRanker, RandomDirectionRanker, TwoStageRandomDirectionRanker and AcceptedRandomDirectionRanker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractRanker"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.rank_key","page":"Emitters","text":"rank_key(ranker::AbstractRanker, ev::Evaluation, result::AddResult)\n\nThe key that orders one candidate: a Float64, or a tuple that compares lexicographically, where a larger key ranks earlier. ev is the evaluation of the candidate and result what the archive made of it. A new ranker defines this method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.rank_key"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.reset_ranker!","page":"Emitters","text":"reset_ranker!(rng, ranker::AbstractRanker, archive)\n\nPrepare ranker for a new search. An emitter calls it when its first search starts and after every restart. The default does nothing; DirectionRankers draw a new direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.reset_ranker!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ObjectiveRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"ObjectiveRanker()\n\nRanks candidates by fitness alone, whatever the archive did with them: the optimizing emitter of CMA-ME. With MuSelection and ConvergenceRestart a CMAEmitter with this ranker is a plain CMA-ES that maximizes the fitness; with the default FilterSelection and NoImprovementRestart it recombines as many of the fittest candidates as the archive accepted and restarts when it accepts none, as in pyribs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ObjectiveRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ImprovementRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"ImprovementRanker()\n\nRanks candidates by the improvement of their AddResult, the gain over the threshold of the cell. This is the ranking of CMA-MAE, for use with a ThresholdArchive.\n\nWith a DensityArchive, whose improvement is the negated density, it ranks the candidates in the sparsest regions of descriptor space first, the ranking of Density Descent Search (DDS).\n\nFor an archive where a new cell reports its fitness as improvement, as the other cell archives do, the improvements of new and old cells are on different scales; TwoStageImprovementRanker is the ranking for those.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ImprovementRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.TwoStageImprovementRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"TwoStageImprovementRanker()\n\nRanks candidates first by what the archive did with them, a new cell above an improved cell above a rejection, and then by improvement. This is the ranking of the improvement emitter of CMA-ME (Fontaine et al., 2020).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.TwoStageImprovementRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.TwoStageObjectiveRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"TwoStageObjectiveRanker()\n\nRanks candidates first by what the archive did with them, as TwoStageImprovementRanker does, and then by fitness. A new cell ranks above an improved cell, whatever the fitness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.TwoStageObjectiveRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.DirectionRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"DirectionRanker\n\nSupertype of the rankers that order candidates by their descriptor projected on a random direction: RandomDirectionRanker, TwoStageRandomDirectionRanker and AcceptedRandomDirectionRanker. The direction is drawn when a search starts, from a standard normal vector scaled by the extent of the archive along each descriptor dimension, so a search that follows it moves toward one part of descriptor space. The archive must report its extent with descriptor_bounds, and its descriptors must be real vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DirectionRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.RandomDirectionRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"RandomDirectionRanker()\n\nRanks candidates by the projection of their descriptor on a random direction, the random direction emitter of CMA-ME (Fontaine et al., 2020). See DirectionRanker. The ranker has the property direction, empty until a search starts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.RandomDirectionRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.TwoStageRandomDirectionRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"TwoStageRandomDirectionRanker()\n\nRanks candidates first by what the archive did with them, as TwoStageImprovementRanker does, and then by the projection of their descriptor on a random direction. A new cell ranks above an improved cell whatever the projection; this is the 2rd ranker of pyribs. See DirectionRanker and, for the ranking that the CMA-ME paper describes, AcceptedRandomDirectionRanker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.TwoStageRandomDirectionRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.AcceptedRandomDirectionRanker","page":"Emitters","text":"AcceptedRandomDirectionRanker()\n\nRanks the candidates that the archive accepted, a new cell or an improvement, above those it rejected, and each group by the projection of the descriptor on a random direction. A new cell and an improved cell are not told apart. This is the random direction emitter as Fontaine et al. (2020) describe it, where only the solutions that improve the archive are ranked by their projection; with FilterSelection the recombined candidates are the accepted ones in that order. See DirectionRanker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AcceptedRandomDirectionRanker"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.SelectionRule","page":"Emitters","text":"SelectionRule\n\nSupertype of the rules that say how many of the ranked candidates a CMAEmitter recombines into the next mean: FilterSelection and MuSelection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.SelectionRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.FilterSelection","page":"Emitters","text":"FilterSelection()\n\nRecombine as many of the first-ranked candidates as the archive accepted, a new cell or an improvement. With a two-stage ranker, which places the accepted candidates first, these are the accepted candidates themselves; with another ranker they are the top-ranked ones, accepted or not. This is the selection of CMA-ME and of pyribs. With no accepted candidate the distribution does not change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.FilterSelection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.MuSelection","page":"Emitters","text":"MuSelection()\n\nRecombine the best half of the candidates, as CMA-ES does. This is the selection of CMA-MAE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.MuSelection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.RestartRule","page":"Emitters","text":"RestartRule\n\nSupertype of the rules that tell a CMAEmitter when to abandon its search and start again from a random elite of the archive: NoImprovementRestart, ConvergenceRestart and PeriodicRestart. Whatever the rule, the emitter also restarts when CMA-ES reports that it has converged or broken down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.RestartRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.NoImprovementRestart","page":"Emitters","text":"NoImprovementRestart()\n\nRestart after a batch in which the archive accepted no candidate. This is the rule of CMA-ME.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.NoImprovementRestart"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ConvergenceRestart","page":"Emitters","text":"ConvergenceRestart()\n\nRestart only when CMA-ES reports that it has converged or broken down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ConvergenceRestart"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.PeriodicRestart","page":"Emitters","text":"PeriodicRestart(iterations)\n\nRestart every iterations batches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.PeriodicRestart"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.GradientArborescenceEmitter","page":"Emitters","text":"GradientArborescenceEmitter(dim; num_descriptors, sigma0, optimizer=AdamAscent(),\n                            batch_size, strategy, ranker, selection, restart, x0,\n                            normalize=true)\nGradientArborescenceEmitter(x0::AbstractVector; num_descriptors, sigma0, ...)\n\nEmitter of CMA-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021, \"Differentiable Quality Diversity\"), for solutions that are real vectors of length dim and problems with num_descriptors descriptor dimensions and gradients; see evaluate_gradient.\n\nThe emitter keeps a solution point θ. Every round starts with the evaluation of θ together with the gradients of its fitness, ∇f, and of its descriptor components, ∇mⱼ, which are normalized to unit length (unless normalize=false), and θ is offered to the archive. The candidates of the round are the branches of a gradient arborescence: θ + c₀∇f + Σⱼ cⱼ∇mⱼ, with the coefficients c drawn from a search distribution of an evolution strategy, CMA-ES by default. The coefficients may take either sign, as in the paper and pyribs. After the round, the candidates are ranked as in a CMAEmitter, the distribution of the coefficients moves toward the best ones, and θ moves by the optimizer along the step Σᵢ wᵢ (c⁽ⁱ⁾ ⋅ ∇) that recombines the selected candidates with the weights of CMA-ES. A round without selected candidates leaves θ where it is.\n\nsigma0: the initial step size of the distribution of the coefficients, which starts at zero. It has no default; the paper uses values from 0.002 to 10.\noptimizer: how θ moves, AdamAscent (the default) or GradientAscent.\nbatch_size, strategy, ranker, selection, restart: as for CMAEmitter, with the strategy searching the num_descriptors + 1 coefficients. The defaults are those of CMA-ME, as in the paper.\nx0: the first θ. Without it, and after every restart, θ is a random elite of the archive, and the distribution of the coefficients starts again from zero.\n\nA round evaluates batch_size + 1 solutions. The loop of step! gets the point from ask_gradients, evaluates it with evaluate_gradient_batch, adds it to the archive and hands the gradients to tell_gradients! before it calls ask; tell! must follow that ask. The emitter has the properties iterations and restarts, as a CMAEmitter. Solutions have no bounds: a step along a gradient has no natural way to respect them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GradientArborescenceEmitter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.GradientOperatorEmitter","page":"Emitters","text":"GradientOperatorEmitter(; sigma0, batch_size=64, normalize=true)\n\nEmitter of OMG-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021, \"Differentiable Quality Diversity\"), MAP-Elites with a gradient step in place of a random mutation. It works on a GradientArchive, which keeps the gradients of every elite. For each candidate it draws an elite θ uniformly over the occupied cells and coefficients c from a normal distribution with mean zero and standard deviation sigma0, takes the absolute value of c₀ so that the step never lowers the fitness to first order, and returns θ + c₀∇f + Σⱼ cⱼ∇mⱼ, with the gradients of the fitness, ∇f, and of the descriptor components, ∇mⱼ, at θ, normalized to unit length unless normalize=false. The paper finds the normalization essential and uses sigma0 from 1 to 10.\n\nsigma0 is the standard deviation of the coefficients, sigma_g in pyribs; QDax takes its square. The solutions must be real vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GradientOperatorEmitter"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ask_gradients","page":"Emitters","text":"ask_gradients(rng::AbstractRNG, emitter::AbstractEmitter, archive::AbstractArchive) -> Union{Nothing,Vector{G}}\n\nSolutions whose gradients the emitter needs before it can ask, or nothing. Optional: the default returns nothing, and an emitter that steps along gradients, such as a GradientArborescenceEmitter, returns its points. Every round, step! evaluates them with evaluate_gradient_batch, adds them to the archive, counts them against the budget, and calls tell_gradients! before it calls ask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ask_gradients"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.tell_gradients!","page":"Emitters","text":"tell_gradients!(emitter::AbstractEmitter, xs, evs, jacobians, results)\n\nReport the solutions xs that ask_gradients returned, their evaluations, their Jacobians (see evaluate_gradient) and the AddResults from the archive. Required for an emitter whose ask_gradients returns solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.tell_gradients!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.GradientOptimizer","page":"Emitters","text":"GradientOptimizer\n\nSupertype of the rules by which a GradientArborescenceEmitter moves its solution point along the step that a round of candidates found: AdamAscent and GradientAscent. They ascend, since fitness is maximized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GradientOptimizer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.AdamAscent","page":"Emitters","text":"AdamAscent(; learning_rate=0.002, beta1=0.9, beta2=0.999, epsilon=1e-8)\n\nMove the solution point with Adam (Kingma and Ba, 2015), as CMA-MEGA (Adam) of Fontaine and Nikolaidis (2021) does: Adam keeps decaying averages of the steps (rate beta1) and of their squares (rate beta2), and moves every entry of the solution by about learning_rate in the direction of the average step, less where the steps disagree. learning_rate = 0.002 is the rate of every experiment of the paper. The update is that of pyribs, with epsilon added to the square root of the second average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AdamAscent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.GradientAscent","page":"Emitters","text":"GradientAscent(learning_rate)\n\nMove the solution point θ by the step Δ of a round, scaled: θ ← θ + learning_rate * Δ. This is the update of CMA-MEGA in Algorithm 1 of Fontaine and Nikolaidis (2021), who use learning_rate = 1. The size of a move follows the size of Δ, so the rate depends on the problem; AdamAscent depends on it less.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GradientAscent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.BanditScheduler","page":"Emitters","text":"BanditScheduler(pool; num_active, zeta=0.05, window=50, reselect=ReselectTerminated())\n\nAn emitter that holds a pool of emitters and runs num_active of them each round, chosen by a multi-armed bandit (Cully, 2021, \"Multi-Emitter MAP-Elites\"; pyribs BanditScheduler). Emitters that keep adding solutions to the archive run more often.\n\npool is a tuple (or a vector) of emitters for the same genotype type. The first round runs the first num_active of them. ask returns the batches of the active emitters one after the other, in the order of the pool, and batch_size is the sum of their batch sizes, so emitters of different batch sizes can share a pool. tell! gives each active emitter its part of the batch and counts, for each emitter, the solutions it produced (selections) and those the archive accepted with NewCell or Improved (successes), over the last window rounds.\n\nThen it chooses the emitters of the next round. reselect says which active emitters leave the active set: ReselectTerminated (the default) or ReselectAll. The free slots go to the inactive emitters, the ones that just left included, with the highest UCB1 score\n\nsuccesses / selections + zeta * sqrt(log(total selections) / selections)\n\nas in Algorithm 1 of the paper. An emitter without selections in the window scores Inf, so every emitter is tried, and a tie goes to the emitter that comes first in the pool. pyribs takes the log of the total successes instead and counts the whole run.\n\nA mutable emitter, such as a CMAEmitter, may appear only once in pool; build a pool of several with a comprehension, not by repeating one instance. tell! must follow the ask that produced the batch. The emitters of the pool must return their batches as a Vector, so device emitters cannot be scheduled.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\narchive = GridArchive(prob; dims=(20, 20))\npool = (VariationEmitter(GaussianMutation(0.1); batch_size=36),\n        VariationEmitter(IsoLineVariation(0.01, 0.1); batch_size=36),\n        (CMAEmitter(10; sigma0=0.5, batch_size=36) for _ in 1:4)...)\nalg = QDAlgorithm(archive, (BanditScheduler(pool; num_active=2),))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=50_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.BanditScheduler"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.active","page":"Emitters","text":"active(scheduler::BanditScheduler) -> Vector{Bool}\n\nWhich emitters of the pool run in the next round, in the order of the pool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.active"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ReselectRule","page":"Emitters","text":"ReselectRule\n\nSupertype of the rules that tell a BanditScheduler which active emitters to take out of the active set after a round: ReselectTerminated and ReselectAll. An emitter taken out competes for the free slots with the inactive ones and may be chosen again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ReselectRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ReselectTerminated","page":"Emitters","text":"ReselectTerminated()\n\nTake an active emitter out of the active set when it restarted during the round, as restarts tells, and always if it cannot restart, such as a VariationEmitter. The default of BanditScheduler, as in pyribs and in Cully (2021).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ReselectTerminated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitters/#QualityDiversity.ReselectAll","page":"Emitters","text":"ReselectAll()\n\nTake every active emitter out of the active set after each round, so that the bandit chooses all of them again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ReselectAll"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/colours/#Colour-pairs-and-emotions","page":"Colour pairs and emotions","text":"Which pairs of colours look striking, and how do they feel? This example fills an archive with pairs of colours, ordered by the emotions they tend to evoke: from calm to arousing, and from sad to happy. The genotype is a pair of colours from Colors.jl, so the example follows the same steps as the graph tutorial: a problem, a variation operator, and MAPElites told about both.\n\nSet up once, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nRun:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/colours.jl\n\nusing Colors: HSV\nusing QualityDiversity\nusing Random: AbstractRNG, Xoshiro","title":"Colour pairs and emotions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/colours/#The-problem","page":"Colour pairs and emotions","text":"A solution is a pair of colours in HSV: hue in degrees, saturation and value between 0 and 1.\n\nconst ColourPair = Tuple{HSV{Float64},HSV{Float64}}\nTuple{ColorTypes.HSV{Float64}, ColorTypes.HSV{Float64}}\n\nThe fitness rewards striking pairs: hues far apart on the colour wheel (180° is complementary), and both colours vivid, that is saturated and bright. It lies between 0 and 1.\n\nhue_distance((a, b)::ColourPair) = min(abs(a.h - b.h), 360 - abs(a.h - b.h))\nvividness((a, b)::ColourPair) = sqrt(a.s * a.v * b.s * b.v)\nfitness(pair::ColourPair) = hue_distance(pair) / 180 * vividness(pair)\nfitness (generic function with 1 method)\n\nThe descriptor places a pair in an emotional space. Valdez and Mehrabian (1994, \"Effects of color on emotions\") found that the brightness and saturation of a colour predict how arousing and how pleasant it feels:\n\narousal = -0.31 brightness + 0.60 saturation,\npleasure = 0.69 brightness + 0.22 saturation.\n\nWe take the value of HSV as the brightness and use the mean over the pair.\n\narousal(s, v) = -0.31 * v + 0.60 * s\npleasure(s, v) = 0.69 * v + 0.22 * s\nmean_sv((a, b)::ColourPair) = ((a.s + b.s) / 2, (a.v + b.v) / 2)\nscore(pair::ColourPair) = (fitness(pair), (arousal(mean_sv(pair)...), pleasure(mean_sv(pair)...)))\nscore (generic function with 1 method)\n\nColours keep a saturation of at least 0.3 and a value of at least 0.4, so that no colour is a grey or nearly black. The bounds of the descriptor follow from those limits.\n\nrandom_colour(rng::AbstractRNG) = HSV(360 * rand(rng), 0.3 + 0.7 * rand(rng), 0.4 + 0.6 * rand(rng))\nrandom_pair(rng::AbstractRNG) = (random_colour(rng), random_colour(rng))\n\nbounds = ((arousal(0.3, 1.0), arousal(1.0, 0.4)), (pleasure(0.3, 0.4), pleasure(1.0, 1.0)))\nprob = QDProblem(score; init=random_pair, bounds)","title":"The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/colours/#The-variation-operator","page":"Colour pairs and emotions","text":"A child changes one of the two colours of its parent by a small random step in hue, saturation and value. The hue wraps around the colour wheel; saturation and value stay within their limits.\n\nstruct ColourMutation <: AbstractVariation end\n\nfunction nudge(rng::AbstractRNG, c::HSV{Float64})\n    return HSV(mod(c.h + 18 * randn(rng), 360),\n               clamp(c.s + 0.05 * randn(rng), 0.3, 1.0),\n               clamp(c.v + 0.05 * randn(rng), 0.4, 1.0))\nend\n\nfunction QualityDiversity.vary(rng::AbstractRNG, ::ColourMutation, (a, b)::ColourPair)\n    return rand(rng, Bool) ? (nudge(rng, a), b) : (a, nudge(rng, b))\nend","title":"The variation operator"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/colours/#Running-MAP-Elites","page":"Colour pairs and emotions","text":"alg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20), batch_size=100, genotype=ColourPair, variation=ColourMutation())\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nQDResult after 100000 evaluations\n  archive: GridArchive with 272 of 400 cells occupied\n  qd_score: 128.73905752305384\n  coverage: 0.68\n  max_fitness: 0.9996836644698366\n  mean_fitness: 0.47330535854063915","title":"Running MAP-Elites"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/colours/#The-archive","page":"Colour pairs and emotions","text":"The heatmap shows the fitness of the best pair in every cell. Vivid colours are bright and saturated, which also makes them pleasant, so the fitness rises toward the top of the archive; the dark, dull pairs at the bottom cannot be vivid.\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig, ax, hm = heatmap(result.archive; figure=(size=(560, 460),),\n                      axis=(xlabel=\"arousal\", ylabel=\"pleasure\", aspect=1))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm; label=\"fitness\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"The archive"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/colours/#Calm-and-arousing,-happy-and-sad","page":"Colour pairs and emotions","text":"The elites at the edges of the archive show what the descriptor means. We sort the elites by arousal and by pleasure and draw the five at each end as swatches.\n\nelites = collect(QualityDiversity.elites(result.archive))\nby_arousal = sort(elites; by=((x, ev),) -> ev.descriptor[1])\nby_pleasure = sort(elites; by=((x, ev),) -> ev.descriptor[2])\n\nfunction swatches!(ax, elites)\n    for (row, (pair, ev)) in enumerate(elites)\n        y = length(elites) - row\n        poly!(ax, Rect(0, y, 1, 0.9); color=pair[1])\n        poly!(ax, Rect(1, y, 1, 0.9); color=pair[2])\n    end\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\n    hidespines!(ax)\n    return ax\nend\n\nfig = Figure(size=(560, 360))\nfor (column, (title, chosen)) in enumerate((\"calmest\" => by_arousal[1:5],\n                                            \"most arousing\" => reverse(by_arousal)[1:5],\n                                            \"saddest\" => by_pleasure[1:5],\n                                            \"happiest\" => reverse(by_pleasure)[1:5]))\n    swatches!(Axis(fig[1, column]; title, aspect=DataAspect()), chosen)\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Calm and arousing, happy and sad"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/algorithms/#Choosing-an-algorithm","page":"Choosing an algorithm","text":"Every algorithm is an archive and a tuple of emitters. The constructors below choose both with the rules of the paper that introduced the algorithm, with one exception: CMAME and CMAMAE run five emitters with the batch size of CMA-ES rather than the 15 emitters of 36 candidates of the papers, which cost about ten times more evaluations per round; pass num_emitters=15, batch_size=36 for the papers' settings. The API reference gives every keyword.","title":"Choosing an algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Choosing an algorithm","text":"Constructor Algorithm Archive Emitters Use it when\nMAPElites MAP-Elites (Mouret and Clune, 2015) GridArchive, or CategoricalArchive for discrete descriptors one VariationEmitter any genotype; the first thing to try\nCMAME CMA-ME (Fontaine et al., 2020) GridArchive five CMAEmitters (15 × 36 in the paper) real-vector genotypes, especially many parameters\nCMAMAE CMA-MAE (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2023) ThresholdArchive around a grid five CMAEmitters (15 × 36 in the paper) as CMA-ME, when fitness should count for more\nCMAMEGA CMA-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021) GridArchive one GradientArborescenceEmitter the fitness and descriptor have gradients\nCMAMAEGA CMA-MAEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2023) ThresholdArchive around a grid one GradientArborescenceEmitter as CMA-MEGA, when fitness should count for more\nOMGMEGA OMG-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021) GradientArchive around a grid one GradientOperatorEmitter gradients, and a simpler search than CMA-MEGA\nDDS Density Descent Search (Lee et al., 2024) DensityArchive around a grid fifteen CMAEmitters exploring descriptor space with little regard for fitness\nMOME Multi-objective MAP-Elites (Pierrot et al., 2022) ParetoGridArchive one VariationEmitter several objectives\nMELS MAP-Elites with low spread (Mace et al., 2023) SpreadArchive around a grid one VariationEmitter noisy descriptors\n\nA few rules of thumb:\n\nStart with MAP-Elites. It works with any genotype for which you can write a variation operator, and its one parameter that matters is the size of the mutation.\nReal vectors with many parameters: the CMA emitters adapt the shape of their search to the problem and usually reach better fitness than a fixed Gaussian mutation. sigma0, the initial step size, has no default because it depends on the scale of the solutions; a tenth to a half of the range of a parameter is a usual start.\nThe optimizer of the CMA emitters is the strategy keyword. CMAES, the default, suits up to a few hundred parameters. Beyond that, SepCMAES and LMMAES cost less per solution. LMMAES works only in a high dimension: it needs at least twice as many parameters as the batch size, so at least 26 with the default batch, and throws otherwise.\nCMA-ME or CMA-MAE: CMA-MAE keeps searching where it has already found good solutions, which CMA-ME abandons once a region is covered. It needs threshold_min, the lowest fitness the problem can produce.\nGradients: when the fitness and the descriptor are differentiable functions of a real vector, the algorithms of differentiable quality diversity step along their gradients and fill an archive in far fewer evaluations than the others. Load ForwardDiff.jl (using ForwardDiff) to compute the gradients of a QDProblem, or define evaluate_gradient for the problem. CMA-MEGA computes the gradients once per round, at one point; OMG-MEGA computes them for every solution, which costs more when a gradient is expensive.\nNoisy problems: wrap the problem in Sampled to average several evaluations; use MELS when the descriptor is noisy too. See the noisy problems tutorial.","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/algorithms/#Archives","page":"Choosing an algorithm","text":"The constructors pick a grid, but any archive can be given to MAPElites, CMAME and the others in place of the problem:\n\narchive = CVTArchive(prob; cells=1_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nalg = MAPElites(archive; batch_size=100)\n\nArchive Cells Use it when\nGridArchive a regular grid over the descriptor bounds few descriptor dimensions (up to about 4)\nCVTArchive the Voronoi cells of centroids from k-means more descriptor dimensions, or a fixed number of cells\nSlidingBoundariesArchive a grid whose boundaries follow the quantiles of recent descriptors descriptors crowd into a small part of their range\nCategoricalArchive one per combination of discrete values descriptors are labels or small integers\nUnstructuredArchive none: a solution enters if it is far from those stored descriptor bounds are unknown (novelty search)\nDNSArchive none: a population chosen by distance to fitter neighbours as above, with a fixed population size\nThresholdArchive those of the archive it wraps CMA-MAE\nSpreadArchive those of the grid or CVT archive it wraps MELS\nDensityArchive those of the archive it wraps DDS\nGradientArchive those of the archive it wraps OMG-MEGA\nParetoGridArchive a grid, with a Pareto front in each cell several objectives\n\nThe algorithm constructors put a descriptor outside the bounds of the grid in the nearest cell, as pyribs and QDax do, so that a random initial population never stops a run. An archive built directly, such as GridArchive(prob; dims), throws instead, which catches a descriptor function that does not match its bounds. Both take outside=ThrowOutside(), outside=ClampOutside() or outside=RejectOutside(), which discards the solution.","title":"Archives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/algorithms/#Variation","page":"Choosing an algorithm","text":"Operator Parents For\nGaussianMutation 1 real vectors\nIsoLineVariation 2 real vectors; moves along the line between two elites (Vassiliades and Mouret, 2018)\nPolynomialMutation 1 real vectors within bounds\nUniformCrossover 2 vectors of any element type\n\nAny other genotype needs an operator of its own; see the graph tutorial.","title":"Variation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/algorithms/#Combining-emitters","page":"Choosing an algorithm","text":"QDAlgorithm takes any archive and any tuple of emitters, for example a Gaussian and an iso-line emitter side by side:\n\narchive = GridArchive(prob; dims=(50, 50))\nemitters = (VariationEmitter(GaussianMutation(0.1); batch_size=50),\n            VariationEmitter(IsoLineVariation(0.01, 0.2); batch_size=50))\nalg = QDAlgorithm(archive, emitters)\n\nAll emitters run every round. A BanditScheduler instead holds a pool of emitters and runs the most successful few each round.","title":"Combining emitters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Archives","page":"Archives","text":"An archive stores the solutions found so far, and decides which of them to keep.","title":"Archives"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Interface","page":"Archives","text":"","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Cell-archives","page":"Archives","text":"","title":"Cell archives"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Descriptors-outside-the-bounds","page":"Archives","text":"","title":"Descriptors outside the bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Archives-around-a-cell-archive","page":"Archives","text":"","title":"Archives around a cell archive"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Archives-without-cells","page":"Archives","text":"","title":"Archives without cells"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Tables","page":"Archives","text":"Loading Tables.jl (for example through DataFrames or CSV) makes every archive a table with one row per elite: DataFrame(result.archive) has the columns genotype, fitness, descriptor_1 to descriptor_N and, for an archive that stores one, phenotype.","title":"Tables"},{"category":"section","location":"api/archives/#Several-objectives","page":"Archives","text":"","title":"Several objectives"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.AbstractArchive","page":"Archives","text":"AbstractArchive{G,F,D,P}\n\nSupertype of archives, which store the best solution found for each region of descriptor space. The type parameters are the genotype G, the fitness F, the descriptor D and the phenotype P, so that code downstream of an archive is concretely typed. P is Nothing for an archive that stores no phenotype; see Evaluation. An archive with a phenotype type other than Nothing stores the phenotype of every solution it takes and throws for an evaluation without one.\n\nRequired methods\n\nadd!(archive, x, ev) → AddResult\ncell_index(archive, d)\nsample_elites(rng, archive, n)\nelites(archive)\ncapacity(archive)\nBase.length(archive): the number of occupied cells\n\nOptional methods\n\nadd_batch!(archive, xs, evs)\nBase.empty!(archive)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.AddStatus","page":"Archives","text":"AddStatus\n\nOutcome of inserting a solution into an archive.\n\nRejected: the archive left unchanged.\nImproved: the solution replaced a worse occupant of its cell.\nNewCell: the solution filled a cell that was empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AddStatus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.AddResult","page":"Archives","text":"AddResult{F}(status, improvement)\n\nResult of add!.\n\nimprovement is the fitness gain over the previous occupant of the cell, or over the archive's threshold for a cell that was empty. It is zero or negative for a rejected solution, and zero for an unsigned fitness type, which has no negative values. A solution the archive has no cell for, one that RejectOutside() discards, gets the lowest improvement there is, -Inf for a floating-point fitness type, so that a ranker places it after every solution that has a cell. Emitters that rank candidates by improvement, such as CMA-ME, read this field. The archives whose improvement means something else, such as DensityArchive and DNSArchive, say so.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AddResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.add!","page":"Archives","text":"add!(archive::AbstractArchive, x, ev::Evaluation) -> AddResult\n\nTry to insert the solution x with evaluation ev into archive. Required.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError, before the archive changes, if the dimension of ev.descriptor does not match the archive or if the fitness is NaN or infinite, also after conversion to the fitness type of the archive. Does not allocate for isbits genotypes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.add!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.add_batch!","page":"Archives","text":"add_batch!(archive::AbstractArchive, xs, evs) -> Vector{<:AddResult}\n\nInsert every solution in xs with its evaluation from evs, returning one AddResult per solution in order. Optional: an archive specializes it when a batch can be inserted faster than one solution at a time.\n\nThe default calls add! on each solution in order. An archive may instead compute every result against its state before the batch, so that a result does not depend on the other solutions in the batch, as the cell archives and ThresholdArchive do; it documents this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.add_batch!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.cell_index","page":"Archives","text":"cell_index(archive::AbstractArchive, d)\n\nThe cell of archive that contains descriptor d. Required. Does not allocate.\n\nAn archive returns 0, which is not a cell, for a descriptor it has no cell for: one that a RejectOutside() policy discards, or any descriptor while an UnstructuredArchive is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.cell_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.sample_elites","page":"Archives","text":"sample_elites(rng::AbstractRNG, archive::AbstractArchive, n) -> Vector{G}\n\nDraw n parent genotypes from the occupied cells of archive. Required. Uniform over occupied cells unless the archive documents otherwise.\n\nThe genotypes are the objects the archive stores, not copies: archives keep what they are given by reference, so that genotypes that are not isbits cost nothing to store or sample. A caller must not modify them; see vary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.sample_elites"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.elites","page":"Archives","text":"elites(archive::AbstractArchive)\n\nIterator over (genotype, evaluation) pairs of the occupied cells. Required. The evaluation holds the stored phenotype, nothing for an archive without one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.elites"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.capacity","page":"Archives","text":"capacity(archive::AbstractArchive)\n\nNumber of cells in archive, occupied or not. Required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.result_archive","page":"Archives","text":"result_archive(archive::AbstractArchive)\n\nThe archive that holds the best solutions found, which solve returns and whose metrics a run records. Optional: the default is archive itself. An archive whose elites can get worse, such as a ThresholdArchive, returns a separate archive that keeps the best solution of each cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.result_archive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.CellArchive","page":"Archives","text":"CellArchive{G,F,D,P}\n\nSupertype of the archives that divide descriptor space into a fixed number of cells and keep the highest-fitness solution found in each: GridArchive, SlidingBoundariesArchive, CVTArchive and CategoricalArchive. They differ in how a descriptor is assigned to a cell and share everything else: storage, add!, add_batch!, sample_elites, elites, length, capacity, empty! and the metrics, including the running totals that make qd_score, max_fitness and mean_fitness constant-time.\n\nA subtype defines cell_index, returning 0 for a descriptor its outside policy rejects, and has these fields, which the shared methods use:\n\noutside: an OutsidePolicy\noccupied::BitVector, fitness::Vector{F}, descriptors::Vector{D}, genotypes::Vector{G}, phenotypes::Vector{P}, occupied_idx::Vector{Int}: one entry per cell\nfitness_sum::Float64, fitness_sum_error::Float64, fitness_max::F: the running totals described under Metrics in GridArchive\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CellArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.GridArchive","page":"Archives","text":"GridArchive{G,F}(dims, bounds; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nGridArchive{G}(dims, bounds; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nGridArchive(dims, bounds; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nGridArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; dims, bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n            fitness=Float64, outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\n\nArchive that divides descriptor space into a regular grid and keeps the highest-fitness solution found in each cell.\n\ndims is a tuple with the number of cells along each descriptor dimension. bounds is a tuple with one (lo, hi) pair per dimension. G is the genotype type, Vector{Float64} by default, and F<:Real the fitness type, Float64 by default. phenotype is the type of the phenotypes the archive stores, Nothing for none; see Evaluation. The last form takes the bounds from descriptor_bounds(prob) unless bounds is given, for example to zoom in on part of descriptor space.\n\nDescriptors are SVector{N,Float64}. outside says what happens to a descriptor that falls outside the bounds:\n\nThrowOutside() (default): throw an ArgumentError.\nClampOutside(): use the nearest cell. A NaN component still throws.\nRejectOutside(): discard the solution; add! returns a Rejected result.\n\nA solution is stored without copying, so the caller must not modify it afterwards.\n\nMetrics\n\ncoverage, qd_score, max_fitness and mean_fitness take constant time: the archive keeps the number of elites, the sum of their fitness and their maximum fitness up to date inside add!, which already knows what changed. A cell is only replaced by a strictly better solution, so the maximum never decreases and a running maximum is exact. The fitness of a new cell is added to the sum, and for an improved cell the new fitness is added and the old one subtracted, each as a step of a compensated (Neumaier) summation in Float64, so the rounding error stays within about one rounding of the exact sum however many updates there have been; a plain running sum drifts by hundreds of units in the last place after millions of updates. The value agrees with a fresh pass over the elites, which the generic metrics for any AbstractArchive perform, to within that rounding.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> archive = GridArchive((10, 10), ((0, 1), (0, 1)));\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GridArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.SlidingBoundariesArchive","page":"Archives","text":"SlidingBoundariesArchive{G,F}(dims, bounds; remap_frequency=100, buffer_capacity=1000,\n                              outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nSlidingBoundariesArchive{G}(dims, bounds; kwargs...)\nSlidingBoundariesArchive(dims, bounds; kwargs...)\nSlidingBoundariesArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; dims, bounds=nothing,\n                         genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, remap_frequency=100,\n                         buffer_capacity=1000, outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\n\nA grid archive whose cell boundaries follow the distribution of the descriptors found (Fontaine et al., 2019, \"Mapping Hearthstone Deck Spaces through MAP-Elites with Sliding Boundaries\"). Where descriptors bunch up, the cells are narrow; where they are rare, the cells are wide.\n\ndims, bounds, G, F and phenotype are those of GridArchive, and the archive starts as a regular grid over bounds. It keeps the genotype, fitness, descriptor and phenotype of the last buffer_capacity solutions offered to it, accepted or not. Every remap_frequency solutions it remaps: along each dimension with n cells, boundary j (for j in 1:n) moves to the ⌊(j-1)·m/n⌋+1-th smallest of the m buffered descriptor values and the upper boundary to the largest, so each cell holds about as many buffered descriptors as the next. It then empties the cells and adds back, one at a time, the old elites and the buffered solutions from oldest to newest, so the best solution found is never lost. A cell can have zero width when several buffered descriptors share a value; it stays empty.\n\nA descriptor in cell j along a dimension lies between boundaries j and j+1; a value on a boundary belongs to the cell above it, and a value on the upper boundary to the last cell. A descriptor outside the boundaries is handled by outside, as for a GridArchive, but the default is ClampOutside(): after a remap the outer boundaries are the extremes of the buffer, so new solutions often land outside them. Old elites are always clamped during a remap. Clamping only chooses the cell: the archive stores the descriptor the problem returned, and a remap places every solution again from it. An elite whose descriptor has left the buffer can lie beyond the new boundaries; it then sits in an edge cell, and its descriptor, as elites returns it, lies outside that cell and outside descriptor_bounds(archive). Every solution that has a cell enters the buffer, so with ClampOutside() the boundaries grow toward descriptors outside them; with RejectOutside() a solution outside the boundaries is discarded for good and never enters the buffer. A descriptor with a non-finite component throws an ArgumentError whatever the policy. The solution that triggers a remap gets its result against the cells before the remap and is then added back from the buffer, so it is not in the archive afterwards if an old elite with a higher fitness lands in its new cell.\n\nadd_batch! computes every result against the archive as it was before the batch, as for the other CellArchives. If the number of offered solutions passes a multiple of remap_frequency during the batch, the archive remaps once, after it. ThresholdArchive and SpreadArchive cannot wrap this archive, since their thresholds and spreads belong to cells that move.\n\nThe current boundaries are returned by boundaries(archive) and their extremes by descriptor_bounds(archive). empty! also clears the buffer and restores the regular grid over bounds.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> archive = SlidingBoundariesArchive((10, 10), ((0, 1), (0, 1)); remap_frequency=50);\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.SlidingBoundariesArchive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.boundaries","page":"Archives","text":"boundaries(archive::SlidingBoundariesArchive) -> NTuple{N,Vector{Float64}}\n\nA copy of the current cell boundaries: for each descriptor dimension with n cells, n + 1 nondecreasing values, cell j lying between entries j and j + 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.boundaries"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.CVTArchive","page":"Archives","text":"CVTArchive{G,F}(centroids, bounds; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nCVTArchive{G,F}(cells, bounds; rng=Random.default_rng(), samples=100_000,\n                max_iterations=100, outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nCVTArchive{G}(...)\nCVTArchive(...)\nCVTArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; cells, rng=Random.default_rng(), bounds=nothing,\n           samples=100_000, max_iterations=100, genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n           fitness=Float64, outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\n\nArchive that divides descriptor space into the Voronoi cells of a set of centroids and keeps the highest-fitness solution found in each cell. A descriptor belongs to the cell of its nearest centroid. Compared with GridArchive, the number of cells does not grow exponentially with the number of descriptor dimensions, and the cells adapt to a measure: with centroids from k-means, a region that the descriptors cover densely gets small cells.\n\nThe centroids are either given, as a vector of descriptor-sized vectors, or chosen from samples points drawn uniformly from the box bounds by k-means (Lloyd's algorithm, seeded with k-means++), which makes the cells equal in expected population. The iterations stop after max_iterations (100) or when one lowers the total squared distance from the samples to their centroids by less than 0.1%. The second form draws its random numbers from rng; the same seed gives the same centroids. For a reproducible run, seed the archive as well as solve, e.g. rng=Xoshiro(1): without it, rng is Random.default_rng(), which the seed of solve does not set. centroids(archive) returns the centroids, and passing them back rebuilds the same archive. The last form takes the bounds from descriptor_bounds(prob) unless bounds is given.\n\nbounds is a tuple with one (lo, hi) pair per descriptor dimension. Given centroids must lie inside the box and be distinct. G is the genotype type, Vector{Float64} by default, and F<:Real the fitness type, Float64 by default. outside says what happens to a descriptor outside the box, as for GridArchive: ThrowOutside(), ClampOutside() or RejectOutside(). phenotype is the type of the phenotypes the archive stores, Nothing for none.\n\nThe nearest centroid is found with a k-d tree, so a lookup takes a logarithmic number of distance evaluations in few dimensions and does not allocate. In many dimensions a k-d tree prunes little, and a lookup approaches a pass over all centroids.\n\nThe archive keeps the same running totals as GridArchive, so the metrics take constant time.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> archive = CVTArchive(50, ((0, 1), (0, 1)); rng=Xoshiro(1), samples=5_000);\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 50)\n\njulia> a = CVTArchive(50, ((0, 1), (0, 1)); samples=5_000);    # no seed\n\njulia> b = CVTArchive(50, ((0, 1), (0, 1)); samples=5_000);\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.centroids(a) == QualityDiversity.centroids(b)    # other cells\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CVTArchive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.centroids","page":"Archives","text":"centroids(archive::CVTArchive) -> Vector{SVector}\n\nThe centroids of the cells, in the order of the cell indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.centroids"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.CategoricalArchive","page":"Archives","text":"CategoricalArchive{G,F}(categories; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nCategoricalArchive{G}(categories; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nCategoricalArchive(categories; outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\nCategoricalArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n                   fitness=Float64, outside=ThrowOutside(), phenotype=Nothing)\n\nArchive with one cell for each combination of discrete descriptor values, and the highest-fitness solution found in each. Use it when a descriptor counts or labels something, such as the number of rings in a molecule or a scaffold class, so that only a few values occur and nothing lies between them.\n\ncategories is a tuple with one collection of allowed values per descriptor dimension, for example (0:5, [:alkane, :aromatic, :ring]). A value can be anything that has hash and isequal: a number, a Symbol, a String, a Bool, or a tuple. Each collection must be non-empty and have distinct values, and a number must be finite. The last form takes the categories from descriptor_categories(prob).\n\nA descriptor is a tuple with one value per dimension, such as (3, :ring). The descriptor type of the archive is the tuple of the element types of categories, here Tuple{Int,Symbol}, so the archive is concretely typed. A vector with one entry per dimension, such as the SVector of a problem with numeric categories, is accepted as well. Values compare with isequal, except that -0.0 equals 0, so 2.0 matches the category 2. A stored descriptor holds the category values.\n\nThe cells are numbered like those of a GridArchive, with the first dimension varying fastest and the values of each dimension in the order given. G is the genotype type, Vector{Float64} by default, and F<:Real the fitness type, Float64 by default. outside says what happens to a descriptor with a component that is not a category:\n\nThrowOutside() (default): throw an ArgumentError.\nRejectOutside(): discard the solution; add! returns a Rejected result.\n\nClampOutside() has no meaning for categories and is an error. phenotype is the type of the phenotypes the archive stores, Nothing for none; see Evaluation.\n\nA category is found with a hash lookup, so a lookup takes constant time and does not allocate however many categories there are. A solution is stored without copying, so the caller must not modify it afterwards. The archive keeps the same running totals as GridArchive, so the metrics take constant time.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> archive = CategoricalArchive((0:5, [:alkane, :aromatic, :ring]));\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 18)\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.cell_index(archive, (3, :ring))\n16\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CategoricalArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.OutsidePolicy","page":"Archives","text":"OutsidePolicy\n\nSupertype of the policies that tell a CellArchive what to do with a descriptor outside its bounds: ThrowOutside(), ClampOutside() and RejectOutside().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.OutsidePolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.ThrowOutside","page":"Archives","text":"Throw an ArgumentError for a descriptor outside the bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ThrowOutside"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.ClampOutside","page":"Archives","text":"Use the nearest cell for a descriptor outside the bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ClampOutside"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.RejectOutside","page":"Archives","text":"Discard a solution whose descriptor is outside the bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.RejectOutside"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.ThresholdArchive","page":"Archives","text":"ThresholdArchive(archive::CellArchive; learning_rate=1.0, threshold_min=-Inf, result=<copy>)\n\nArchive for CMA-MAE (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2023): a cell archive in which each cell has a fitness threshold that rises slowly as solutions enter the cell, instead of jumping to the best fitness found. archive decides which cell a descriptor belongs to, so any CellArchive works: a GridArchive, a CVTArchive or a CategoricalArchive. It must be empty and have a floating-point fitness type, and the new archive uses it without copying.\n\nA solution with fitness f enters its cell if f is strictly greater than the cell's threshold t, and then replaces the cell's elite. The threshold becomes (1 - learning_rate) * t + learning_rate * f. The improvement of the AddResult is f - t, which is what CMA-ME style emitters rank candidates by; for a rejected solution it is zero or negative. Every threshold starts at threshold_min, and a solution can only enter an empty cell if its fitness exceeds that value. A good threshold_min is the lowest fitness the problem can produce, and learning_rate=0.01 is a usual choice.\n\nThe elite of a cell is the solution that last passed the threshold, so its fitness can be lower than the one it replaced when learning_rate < 1: a solution above the threshold but below the elite replaces the elite. The archive therefore does not hold the best solution found in each cell. A second, plain archive does: every solution offered to this archive is offered to it as well, and result_archive returns it. Emitters read this archive, and a run reports the metrics of, and returns, the result archive. The result keyword takes that archive; by default it is an empty copy of the wrapped one, and it is nothing when learning_rate=1 and threshold_min=-Inf, where this archive already holds the best solutions and is its own result. It must be empty and of the same type as the wrapped archive.\n\nlearning_rate must lie in [0, 1]. With learning_rate=0 the thresholds never change, and with learning_rate=1 a threshold becomes the fitness of the solution that entered. To keep the behavior of an archive with another number of cells, convert the rate with 1 - (1 - learning_rate)^r, where r is the ratio of the cell counts.\n\nThe defaults, learning_rate=1 and threshold_min=-Inf, give the rules of the wrapped archive: an empty cell accepts any solution and reports improvement == f, and the threshold of a cell is the fitness of its elite. That setting needs learning_rate=1, so threshold_min=-Inf with a smaller rate is an error.\n\nadd_batch! computes the status and improvement of every solution against the archive as it was before the batch, so a solution does not depend on the others in its batch. Of the solutions that pass the threshold of a cell, the one with the highest fitness (the first, on a tie) becomes the elite. The threshold of a cell that m solutions of the batch passed becomes (1 - learning_rate)^m * t + (1 - (1 - learning_rate)^m) * mean(f), where mean(f) is over those solutions, or the highest of their fitness values if threshold_min is -Inf. For a single solution this is the rule above, which is what add! applies. thresholds returns the thresholds, one per cell.\n\nCells, sampling, elites, length, capacity, cell_index and the stored phenotypes are those of the wrapped archive, and so are the metrics, which describe the elites of this archive and not the result archive. qd_score and mean_fitness take constant time. max_fitness does too, except directly after the best elite was replaced by a worse solution, when the next call makes a pass over the elites.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> archive = ThresholdArchive(GridArchive((10, 10), ((0, 1), (0, 1)));\n                                  learning_rate=0.5, threshold_min=0);\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ThresholdArchive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.thresholds","page":"Archives","text":"thresholds(archive::ThresholdArchive) -> Vector\n\nThe fitness threshold of each cell, in the order of the cell indices. A cell that has never accepted a solution holds threshold_min.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.thresholds"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.SpreadArchive","page":"Archives","text":"SpreadArchive(archive::Union{GridArchive,CVTArchive})\n\nArchive for MAP-Elites with low spread (Mace et al., 2023, \"The Quality-Diversity Transformer\"), the archive of MELS: a cell archive that prefers, among solutions of a noisy problem, those whose evaluations land close together. archive decides which cell a descriptor belongs to; it must be empty, and the new archive uses it without copying.\n\nThe archive takes the evaluations of a SampledSpread problem, whose descriptor holds the descriptors of all the evaluations of a solution. From them it finds\n\nthe cell of the solution: the one its descriptors land in most often, the one visited first on a tie;\nits spread: the mean distance between two of its descriptors, zero for a single one;\nits stored descriptor: the mean of its descriptors in that cell, which lies inside the cell.\n\nThe fitness is the one of the evaluation, the mean over the evaluations by default. An empty cell takes any solution (NewCell, with improvement equal to the fitness). An occupied cell takes a solution only if its fitness is strictly higher and its spread no larger than those of the elite (Improved, with improvement the fitness gain); otherwise the solution is Rejected, with the fitness gain as improvement if it is negative and zero if the spread was the reason. A solution whose descriptors all fall outside the bounds of a RejectOutside() archive, or whose spread is not finite, is Rejected with zero improvement.\n\nadd_batch! computes the status and improvement of every solution against the archive as it was before the batch, and the archive afterwards is the one that adding the solutions one at a time gives. spreads returns the spread of every cell. Cells, sampling, elites, length, capacity, the stored phenotypes and the metrics are those of the wrapped archive, which stay exact because an elite is only replaced by a solution with higher fitness.\n\nA CategoricalArchive cannot be wrapped: its descriptors have no distance.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, StaticArrays\n\njulia> archive = SpreadArchive(GridArchive((2, 2), ((0, 1), (0, 1))));\n\njulia> samples = QualityDiversity.DescriptorSamples([SVector(0.1, 0.1), SVector(0.2, 0.1), SVector(0.9, 0.9)]);\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.add!(archive, [0.0], Evaluation(1.0, samples)).status\nNewCell::AddStatus = 2\n\njulia> only(QualityDiversity.elites(archive))[2].descriptor\n2-element SVector{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n 0.15000000000000002\n 0.1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.SpreadArchive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.spreads","page":"Archives","text":"spreads(archive::SpreadArchive) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe spread of the elite of every cell, in the order of the cell indices; NaN for an empty cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.spreads"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.DensityArchive","page":"Archives","text":"DensityArchive(inner::AbstractArchive; bandwidth, buffer_capacity=10_000,\n               rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nThe archive of Density Descent Search (Lee et al., 2024, \"Density Descent for Diversity Optimization\"; pyribs DensityArchive). It estimates how densely the descriptors found so far cover descriptor space, so that emitters can move toward where they are sparse.\n\nEvery solution goes to inner, an archive with SVector descriptors such as a GridArchive, which holds the solutions: solve returns it, a run records its metrics, and emitters draw their parents and restarts from it. The descriptors also go to a buffer of buffer_capacity entries that stays a uniform sample of all the descriptors seen, by reservoir sampling (Li, 1994, Algorithm L) with the generator rng. The archive keeps its own copy of rng, since add! takes no generator; init copies the archive, so a run is reproducible from the seed of rng and the seed of the run. For a reproducible run, seed the archive as well as solve, e.g. rng=Xoshiro(1): without it, rng is Random.default_rng(), which the seed of solve does not set.\n\nThe density at a descriptor y is the Gaussian kernel density estimate of the buffer,\n\nsum(φ(norm(y - b) / h) for b in buffer) / (length(buffer) * h)\n\nwith φ the standard normal density and h = bandwidth, in descriptor units, and zero while the buffer is empty. bandwidth has no default; the paper finds about 5% of the width of descriptor space a good start. A result has the status that inner gives the solution and improvement = -density, so that ImprovementRanker ranks the lowest density first. Every result of a batch is computed against the buffer as it was before the batch. A density takes time linear in the buffer; add_batch! computes those of a batch on several threads when the batch is large enough to pay for them, with the same results whatever the number of threads.\n\nresult_archive returns inner, and length, capacity, cell_index, sample_elites, elites, descriptor_bounds and the metrics are those of inner. empty! empties inner and the buffer. The fitness type must be a floating-point type, for the density. DDS builds the algorithm.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> archive = DensityArchive(GridArchive((10, 10), ((0, 1), (0, 1))); bandwidth=0.05, rng=Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DensityArchive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.density","page":"Archives","text":"density(archive::DensityArchive, d) -> Float64\n\nThe kernel density estimate of the buffer at descriptor d; zero while the buffer is empty. See DensityArchive. Throws an ArgumentError if d has the wrong number of dimensions or a component that is not finite, which would make every later density NaN once in the buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.density"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.GradientArchive","page":"Archives","text":"GradientArchive(archive)\n\nArchive for OMG-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021): a cell archive that also keeps, next to every elite, the gradients of its fitness and descriptor, which the GradientOperatorEmitter steps along. archive decides everything else: which cell a descriptor belongs to and which solution a cell keeps. It is a GridArchive, a CVTArchive or a ThresholdArchive around one, with SVector descriptors; it must be empty, and the new archive uses it without copying.\n\nEvery solution offered to this archive comes with its Jacobian, an (N + 1) × n matrix for a solution of n entries and N descriptor dimensions, as evaluate_gradient returns it. A run with this archive therefore evaluates every solution with its gradients, the initial ones included, and counts each as one evaluation. The paper does the same; pyribs instead evaluates the gradients of a parent again when it is drawn, and QDax keeps them as this archive does.\n\nadd! and add_batch! take the Jacobians as a fourth argument, add!(archive, x, ev, jacobian) and add_batch!(archive, xs, evs, jacobians), and throw without them; the results are those of the wrapped archive. A cell keeps the Jacobian of the solution that the wrapped archive keeps: of the solutions of a batch that enter a cell, the one with the highest fitness (the first on a tie). The memory is that of N + 1 vectors as long as a solution, for every occupied cell.\n\nCells, sampling, elites, length, capacity, cell_index, the metrics and result_archive are those of the wrapped archive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.GradientArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.UnstructuredArchive","page":"Archives","text":"UnstructuredArchive{G,F}(ndims; capacity, novelty_threshold, phenotype=Nothing)\nUnstructuredArchive{G}(ndims; capacity, novelty_threshold, phenotype=Nothing)\nUnstructuredArchive(ndims; capacity, novelty_threshold, phenotype=Nothing)\nUnstructuredArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; capacity, novelty_threshold,\n                    genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, phenotype=Nothing)\n\nArchive without a fixed partition of descriptor space. It stores up to capacity solutions, each at its own descriptor, and a solution enters if it is far from the ones already stored (novelty) or better than its nearest neighbour (local competition).\n\nndims is the number of descriptor dimensions; the last form takes it from length(descriptor_bounds(prob)). G is the genotype type, Vector{Float64} by default, and F<:Real the fitness type, Float64 by default. Descriptors are SVector{ndims,Float64}, and distances are Euclidean in descriptor units, so descriptors on very different scales should be normalized by the problem. novelty_threshold is the distance beyond which a descriptor counts as new, as in pyribs. phenotype is the type of the phenotypes the archive stores, Nothing for none; see Evaluation.\n\nadd! finds the stored solution with the nearest descriptor, at distance r:\n\nNothing is stored, or r > novelty_threshold: the solution is novel. It fills a free slot (NewCell, with improvement equal to the fitness). If the archive is full, it replaces the stored solution with the lowest fitness, but only if its fitness is strictly higher (NewCell); otherwise it is Rejected.\nr <= novelty_threshold: the solution competes with its nearest neighbour and replaces it if its fitness is strictly higher (Improved, with improvement the difference), and is Rejected otherwise. The new descriptor replaces the old one, so stored descriptors can end up closer together than novelty_threshold.\n\ncell_index is the index of the stored solution with the nearest descriptor, or 0 while the archive is empty. capacity is the number of slots, so coverage is the fraction of them in use. Descriptors must be finite; a NaN or infinite component throws an ArgumentError.\n\nWith fewer than max(64, 4^ndims) stored solutions the nearest neighbour is found by a pass over the stored descriptors. From that many on, add! keeps a k-d tree of the stored descriptors, which it rebuilds when the solutions stored since the last build number more than 64 + 8 sqrt(n) for n stored solutions, and a search checks those solutions one by one. Both find the same neighbour, and neither allocates. The solution of lowest fitness, which a full archive replaces, is kept on top of a heap. The archive has no add_batch! of its own: the default inserts the solutions of a batch one at a time with add!, so the result of a solution depends on the solutions before it in the batch, unlike in a GridArchive. A solution is stored without copying, so the caller must not modify it afterwards. The archive keeps running totals, as GridArchive does, so qd_score, max_fitness and mean_fitness take constant time. The maximum is exact because a stored solution is only ever replaced by a better one.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> archive = UnstructuredArchive(2; capacity=100, novelty_threshold=0.1);\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.UnstructuredArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.DNSArchive","page":"Archives","text":"DNSArchive{G,F}(ndims; capacity, k, phenotype=Nothing)\nDNSArchive{G}(ndims; capacity, k, phenotype=Nothing)\nDNSArchive(ndims; capacity, k, phenotype=Nothing)\nDNSArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; capacity, k, genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n           fitness=Float64, phenotype=Nothing)\n\nArchive of Dominated Novelty Search (Bahlous-Boldi, Faldor et al., 2025, \"Dominated Novelty Search: Rethinking Local Competition in Quality-Diversity\"; pyribs DNSArchive, QDax DominatedNoveltyRepertoire). It keeps a population of at most capacity solutions, with no cells and no bounds on descriptor space.\n\nndims is the number of descriptor dimensions; the last form takes it from length(descriptor_bounds(prob)). G is the genotype type, Vector{Float64} by default, and F<:AbstractFloat the fitness type, Float64 by default. Descriptors are SVector{ndims,Float64}, and distances are Euclidean in descriptor units, so descriptors on very different scales should be normalized by the problem. phenotype is the type of the phenotypes the archive stores, Nothing for none; see Evaluation.\n\nadd_batch! merges the batch into the population and gives every solution its dominated novelty: the mean distance to its k nearest other solutions whose fitness is at least as high (all of them if there are fewer), or Inf if there is none. The capacity solutions with the highest scores survive; on a tie, a solution of the batch goes before a stored one, and a later one before an earlier one. A solution of the batch that survives is NewCell and one that does not is Rejected, and its improvement is its score. Since the population is chosen as a whole, the result of a solution depends on the rest of the batch. A neighbour of equal fitness counts as fitter, as in pyribs and QDax, so solutions on a plateau of fitness still compete on distance; the paper counts strictly fitter neighbours only. add! is a batch of one solution.\n\ncell_index is the index of the stored solution with the nearest descriptor, or 0 while the archive is empty, and capacity the population size, so coverage is the fraction of it in use. Descriptors must be finite; a NaN or infinite component throws an ArgumentError. With at least 16 * 4^ndims solutions in the population and the batch together, scoring searches a k-d tree of them, built anew for every batch, that knows the highest fitness in each of its branches, so a search skips the branches without a solution at least as fit; with fewer, where the tree gains nothing, it takes a pass over all of them. Both give the same scores. Scoring keeps only k distances per solution and allocates nothing once the workspace fits the batch. max_fitness and mean_fitness can decrease, since a solution can leave the population.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> archive = DNSArchive(2; capacity=100, k=3);\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.capacity(archive)\n(0, 100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DNSArchive"},{"category":"module","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversityTablesExt","page":"Archives","text":"QualityDiversityTablesExt\n\nTables.jl support for archives. With Tables loaded, every AbstractArchive is a table with one row per elite of its result_archive, in the order of elites, so DataFrame(archive) and Tables.rowtable(archive) work. A ThresholdArchive thus shows the best solution found in each cell, as its heatmap does. The columns are\n\ngenotype: a copy of the stored genotype, so changing it leaves the archive as it is;\nfitness: the fitness, a single column also for a vector of objectives;\ndescriptor_1, ..., descriptor_N: one column per descriptor dimension, for an SVector or a tuple descriptor;\nphenotype: a copy of the stored phenotype, only for an archive that stores one.\n\nThe column types follow from the type parameters of the archive, so an empty archive has the same columns as a full one. An archive whose descriptor type has no length in its type, such as a Vector, throws an ArgumentError.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, StaticArrays, Tables\n\njulia> archive = GridArchive((2, 2), ((0, 1), (0, 1)));\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.add!(archive, [0.5], Evaluation(1.0, SVector(0.25, 0.75)));\n\njulia> Tables.columntable(archive)\n(genotype = [[0.5]], fitness = [1.0], descriptor_1 = [0.25], descriptor_2 = [0.75])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversityTablesExt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.ParetoGridArchive","page":"Archives","text":"ParetoGridArchive{G}(dims, bounds; reference_point, max_front=50, outside=ThrowOutside(),\n                     duplicates=RejectDuplicates(), full_front=RejectNewcomer(),\n                     phenotype=Nothing)\nParetoGridArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; dims, reference_point, max_front=50,\n                  bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n                  outside=ThrowOutside(), duplicates=RejectDuplicates(),\n                  full_front=RejectNewcomer(), phenotype=Nothing)\n\nArchive for several objectives, the one of MOME (Pierrot et al., 2022): the cells are those of a GridArchive, and every cell keeps a Pareto front, the solutions of the cell that no other solution of the cell beats in every objective, instead of one elite.\n\ndims is the number of cells along each descriptor dimension and bounds the box that they cover, as for a GridArchive. The fitness of a solution is a vector of M objectives, all maximized, and reference_point, a vector of M numbers, is the corner from which hypervolumes are measured. It should lie below the objective values that matter, since a solution contributes hypervolume only where it is larger than the reference point in every objective. A cell keeps at most max_front solutions. The archive stores its fitness values as SVector{M,Float64}. phenotype is the type of the phenotypes the archive stores, Nothing for none; see Evaluation.\n\nadd! puts a solution into the front of its cell unless a solution there is at least as good in every objective and better in one; it removes the solutions that the new one beats in every objective. duplicates decides about a solution whose objectives equal those of a stored one: RejectDuplicates (the default) or KeepDuplicates. full_front decides about a solution that beats none of a full front: RejectNewcomer (the default, as in QDax) rejects it and ReplaceCrowded removes the most crowded of the front and the new solution. The result is NewCell for a cell that was empty and Improved otherwise, and its improvement is the hypervolume that the cell gains, a Float64; a rejected solution has an improvement of zero. The results of add_batch! are those of each solution against the archive as it was before the batch, and the solutions are then added in order.\n\nlength(archive) is the number of occupied cells, as for the other archives, and num_solutions the number of solutions. elites iterates over all of them. sample_elites draws an occupied cell uniformly and then a solution of its front uniformly.\n\nMetrics\n\nqd_score is the MOQD score, the sum over the cells of the hypervolume of their fronts; it takes no offset. coverage is the fraction of cells that are occupied, max_hypervolume the largest hypervolume of a cell and global_hypervolume the hypervolume of the Pareto front of all the solutions. max_fitness and mean_fitness are not defined for several objectives.\n\nThe hypervolume of a front is computed, without allocating, when a solution is offered to its cell, and once more in a batch for a solution whose cell an earlier solution of the batch changed.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, StaticArrays\n\njulia> archive = ParetoGridArchive{Vector{Float64}}((2, 2), ((0, 1), (0, 1)); reference_point=[0.0, 0.0]);\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.add!(archive, [0.1], Evaluation(SVector(1.0, 3.0), SVector(0.2, 0.2))).status\nNewCell::AddStatus = 2\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.add!(archive, [0.2], Evaluation(SVector(3.0, 1.0), SVector(0.2, 0.2))).improvement\n2.0\n\njulia> length(archive), QualityDiversity.num_solutions(archive), qd_score(archive)\n(1, 2, 5.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ParetoGridArchive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.num_solutions","page":"Archives","text":"num_solutions(archive)\n\nNumber of solutions in archive. For a ParetoGridArchive, which keeps a front in every cell, it is larger than length(archive), the number of occupied cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.num_solutions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.DuplicatePolicy","page":"Archives","text":"DuplicatePolicy\n\nSupertype of the rules that decide whether a ParetoGridArchive takes a solution whose objectives equal those of a solution in the front of its cell: RejectDuplicates and KeepDuplicates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DuplicatePolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.RejectDuplicates","page":"Archives","text":"RejectDuplicates()\n\nReject a solution whose objectives equal those of a solution in the front of its cell, so that a front holds every objective vector once. The default of ParetoGridArchive. The MOME paper and QDax's MOMERepertoire keep both; see KeepDuplicates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.RejectDuplicates"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.KeepDuplicates","page":"Archives","text":"KeepDuplicates()\n\nTake a solution whose objectives equal those of a solution in the front of its cell, as the MOME paper and QDax's MOMERepertoire do: only a solution that is at least as good in every objective and better in one keeps it out. Different genotypes with equal objectives then all stay, in slots of the front that add no hypervolume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.KeepDuplicates"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.FullFrontPolicy","page":"Archives","text":"FullFrontPolicy\n\nSupertype of the rules that decide what a ParetoGridArchive does with a solution for a full front, one that holds max_front solutions of which the new one beats none in every objective: RejectNewcomer and ReplaceCrowded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.FullFrontPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.RejectNewcomer","page":"Archives","text":"RejectNewcomer()\n\nReject the solution, so that a full front changes only when a new solution beats one of its solutions in every objective. The default of ParetoGridArchive, as in QDax's MOMERepertoire; the text of the MOME paper replaces a random solution of the front instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.RejectNewcomer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/archives/#QualityDiversity.ReplaceCrowded","page":"Archives","text":"ReplaceCrowded()\n\nOf the solutions of the front and the new one, remove the one with the smallest crowding distance, so that the front stays spread out, as MOME-PGX does (Janmohamed, Pierrot and Cully, 2023, \"Improving the Data Efficiency of Multi-Objective Quality-Diversity through Gradient Assistance and Crowding Exploration\").\n\nThe crowding distance is that of NSGA-II: for each objective, the gap between the two neighbours of a solution in that objective divided by the range of the objective over these solutions, summed over the objectives. The solution with the smallest value of an objective and the one with the largest have an infinite distance, so the ends of the front are never removed; when several solutions share an extreme value, only one of them is protected in that objective, the first stored among those at the smallest value and the last stored among those at the largest, and the others get a finite distance like any solution between. An objective in which all of them are equal adds nothing. On a tie the new solution is removed, and otherwise the one stored first among those tied.\n\nWhen the new solution stays, its result is Improved and its improvement is the change in the hypervolume of the cell, which is negative when the removed solution covered more.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.ReplaceCrowded"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rastrigin_tutorial/#MAP-Elites-on-the-Rastrigin-function","page":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function","text":"Quality-diversity (QD) optimization does not look for one best solution. It fills a grid of cells, each covering a region of descriptor space, with the best solution found for that cell. This tutorial goes through the workflow of the package once:\n\ndefine a problem,\nchoose an algorithm,\nrun it with solve, and\nlook at the result: metrics, elites and a heatmap.\n\nSet up once, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nRun:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/rastrigin_tutorial.jl\n\nusing QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nusing Random: Xoshiro","title":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rastrigin_tutorial/#1.-The-problem","page":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function","text":"Rastrigin(10), from QualityDiversity.Benchmarks, is a benchmark over 10 real parameters. The fitness of a solution is the negated Rastrigin value, so higher is better, with a maximum of 0. Its descriptor is two numbers: the mean of the first five parameters and the mean of the last five, each in [-5.12, 5.12].\n\nprob = Rastrigin(10)\nQualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\n\nFor your own problem, QDProblem(f; init, bounds) wraps a function f that returns (fitness, descriptor); see the graph tutorial.","title":"1. The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rastrigin_tutorial/#2.-The-algorithm","page":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function","text":"MAP-Elites keeps a grid over descriptor space, here 20 by 20 cells. Each round it draws batch_size parents from the grid, mutates them with Gaussian noise of standard deviation 0.3, and a child takes a cell if it is empty or if the child beats the solution there.\n\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20), batch_size=100, variation=GaussianMutation(0.3))","title":"2. The algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rastrigin_tutorial/#3.-Running-it","page":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function","text":"The budget counts evaluations. A seeded random number generator makes the run reproducible.\n\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nQDResult after 100000 evaluations\n  archive: GridArchive with 317 of 400 cells occupied\n  qd_score: 91262.10642648741\n  coverage: 0.7925\n  max_fitness: -36.09277954655117\n  mean_fitness: -122.10691979026055","title":"3. Running it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rastrigin_tutorial/#4.-The-result","page":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function","text":"solve returns a summary of the final archive. result.history holds the same metrics after every round, so you can follow the run, from the first round to the last:\n\nresult.history[1]\n(n_evals = 100, qd_score = 14693.114949369326, coverage = 0.16, max_fitness = -113.84875070095471, mean_fitness = -180.42007891610427)\n\nresult.history[end]\n(n_evals = 100000, qd_score = 91262.10642648741, coverage = 0.7925, max_fitness = -36.09277954655117, mean_fitness = -122.10691979026055)\n\nresult.archive is the grid. Its elites, the best solution of every filled cell, come as pairs of a solution and its evaluation:\n\narchive = result.archive\nelites = collect(QualityDiversity.elites(archive))\nlength(elites)\n317\n\nx, ev = first(elites)\n(fitness=ev.fitness, descriptor=ev.descriptor)\n(fitness = -116.41388161481927, descriptor = [-2.182748934508153, 1.0771716735650352])\n\nThe best elite of the whole archive:\n\nbest_x, best_ev = argmax(((x, ev),) -> ev.fitness, elites)\nround.(best_x; digits=2)\n10-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.98\n -0.14\n  1.06\n -1.11\n  1.17\n -0.03\n -0.07\n  2.21\n  0.82\n -0.02\n\nWith a Makie backend loaded, such as CairoMakie, heatmap draws the fitness of every cell; empty cells stay blank. Plotting archives shows more.\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig, ax, hm = heatmap(archive; figure=(size=(560, 460),),\n                      axis=(xlabel=\"descriptor 1\", ylabel=\"descriptor 2\", aspect=1))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm; label=\"fitness\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"4. The result"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rastrigin_tutorial/#Things-to-try","page":"MAP-Elites on the Rastrigin function","text":"A different mutation size: GaussianMutation(0.1) or GaussianMutation(1.0).\nA crossover that moves along the line between two elites: variation=IsoLineVariation(0.01, 0.2).\nA finer grid: dims=(40, 40).\nStepping one round at a time instead of calling solve: state = init(prob, alg; budget=10_000, rng=Xoshiro(1)); step!(state).\nAnother algorithm, such as CMAME(prob; dims=(20, 20), sigma0=0.5): the rest of the workflow stays the same.","title":"Things to try"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Extending-the-package","page":"Extending the package","text":"Every component plays one of five roles, and each role is an abstract type with a short contract: the functions a new type must define, and those it may define. A type that meets its contract works with every other component and with the generic loop. QualityDiversity.Testing checks that it does.\n\nExtend the interface functions with their qualified names, as you would Base.show:\n\nQualityDiversity.evaluate(prob::MyProblem, x) = ...","title":"Extending the package"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#A-problem","page":"Extending the package","text":"QDProblem covers most problems. A type of your own is useful when the problem has parameters, or to evaluate a batch at once. It defines three functions:\n\nusing QualityDiversity\nusing Random: AbstractRNG, Xoshiro\nusing StaticArrays: SVector\n\n\"Points in the unit square; fitness is closeness to `target`.\"\nstruct Target <: AbstractQDProblem\n    target::NTuple{2,Float64}\nend\n\nfunction QualityDiversity.evaluate(prob::Target, x)\n    point = clamp.(SVector(x[1], x[2]), 0.0, 1.0)     # the descriptor stays within its bounds\n    fitness = -hypot(point[1] - prob.target[1], point[2] - prob.target[2])\n    return Evaluation(fitness, point)\nend\n\nQualityDiversity.random_solution(rng::AbstractRNG, ::Target) = rand(rng, 2)\nQualityDiversity.descriptor_bounds(::Target) = ((0.0, 1.0), (0.0, 1.0))\n\nAn Evaluation holds the fitness, the descriptor, an SVector from StaticArrays.jl (or a tuple, for categories), and an optional phenotype. Optional functions are evaluate_batch, to score a batch at once, fitness_offset, for the QD score, and reference_point, for several objectives. test_problem checks the contract, including that evaluate is inferrable and the descriptors lie within the bounds:\n\nusing QualityDiversity.Testing\ntest_problem(Target((0.3, 0.7)))\nTest Summary:        | Pass  Total  Time\ntest_problem(Target) |   41     41  0.1s\n\nThe default evaluate_batch calls evaluate on several threads, so evaluate must be thread-safe: it must not write to shared state without a lock.","title":"A problem"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#A-genotype-and-its-variation","page":"Extending the package","text":"A genotype can be any Julia type. The archives store it as it is, and only the variation operator looks inside: define an operator type and a vary method that returns a new child and leaves the parent unchanged. The graph tutorial does this for Graphs.SimpleGraph. Here is a mutation for strings:\n\nstruct LetterMutation <: AbstractVariation end\n\nfunction QualityDiversity.vary(rng::AbstractRNG, ::LetterMutation, parent::String)\n    letters = collect(parent)\n    letters[rand(rng, eachindex(letters))] = rand(rng, 'a':'z')\n    return String(letters)\nend\n\ntest_variation(LetterMutation(), \"quality\")\nTest Summary:                  | Pass  Total  Time\ntest_variation(LetterMutation) |    5      5  0.0s\n\nA crossover defines vary(rng, op, p1, p2) and n_parents(op) = 2. Tell the algorithm the genotype type, so that the archive is concretely typed: MAPElites(prob; dims, genotype=String, variation=LetterMutation()).","title":"A genotype and its variation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#An-emitter","page":"Extending the package","text":"An emitter defines ask, which returns a batch of candidates, and batch_size. It may define tell! to learn from the results; the default does nothing. This one mutates only the fittest elites:\n\n\"Mutate parents drawn from the `top` fittest elites.\"\nstruct GreedyEmitter{V<:AbstractVariation} <: AbstractEmitter\n    op::V\n    top::Int\n    batch_size::Int\nend\n\nQualityDiversity.batch_size(e::GreedyEmitter) = e.batch_size\n\nfunction QualityDiversity.ask(rng::AbstractRNG, e::GreedyEmitter, archive::AbstractArchive{G}) where {G}\n    ranked = sort!(collect(QualityDiversity.elites(archive)); by=elite -> elite[2].fitness, rev=true)\n    parents = first.(ranked[1:min(e.top, length(ranked))])\n    return G[QualityDiversity.vary(rng, e.op, rand(rng, parents)) for _ in 1:e.batch_size]\nend\n\nprob = Target((0.3, 0.7))\narchive = GridArchive(prob; dims=(10, 10))\nalg = QDAlgorithm(archive, (VariationEmitter(GaussianMutation(0.05); batch_size=20),\n                            GreedyEmitter(GaussianMutation(0.01), 5, 20)))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=5_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nmax_fitness(result.archive)\n-0.0001747640584217743\n\ntest_emitter checks the contract against an archive that holds a few solutions. The batch must be a Vector of the archive's genotype type, and ask must be inferrable, which the G[...] comprehension above ensures.\n\nAn emitter with state is a mutable struct, or holds mutable fields; init copies the emitters, so a run never changes the algorithm it was given.","title":"An emitter"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#An-archive","page":"Extending the package","text":"An archive is the most involved role. It defines add!, which returns an AddResult with the status (NewCell, Improved or Rejected) and the improvement over the previous occupant, cell_index, sample_elites, elites, capacity and Base.length, the number of occupied cells. The type parameters of AbstractArchive{G,F,D,P} are the genotype, fitness, descriptor and phenotype types, which emitters use to build concretely typed batches.\n\nA new kind of cell for real-valued descriptors does not need a new archive: subtypes of CellArchive share the storage, insertion and metrics, and differ only in cell_index. Read the source of GridArchive for a small example, and check the result with test_archive, which also checks that cell_index and add! do not allocate.","title":"An archive"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Checking-conformance-in-your-tests","page":"Extending the package","text":"The checks of QualityDiversity.Testing run inside a @testset, so they report to the test runner of the package that calls them:\n\nusing Test, QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Testing\n\n@testset \"MyPackage\" begin\n    test_problem(MyProblem())\n    test_variation(MyMutation(), example_genotype)\n    test_archive(() -> MyArchive(...), MyProblem())\nend","title":"Checking conformance in your tests"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Concepts","page":"Concepts","text":"Quality-diversity (QD) optimization looks for many good solutions that differ from each other in ways the user cares about, not for one best solution. Each solution has a fitness, how good it is, and a descriptor, a few numbers (or labels) that say what kind of solution it is. An archive divides descriptor space into regions and keeps the best solution found in each, its elite. A run fills the archive.\n\nThis page describes how the package is built from five roles, how a run proceeds, and how to read its results.","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#The-five-roles","page":"Concepts","text":"Role Abstract type Its job Examples\nProblem AbstractQDProblem score and describe a solution, draw a random one QDProblem, Rastrigin\nArchive AbstractArchive decide which solutions to keep GridArchive, CVTArchive\nVariation AbstractVariation make a child from one or two parents GaussianMutation\nEmitter AbstractEmitter propose a batch of candidates each round VariationEmitter, CMAEmitter\nAlgorithm QDAlgorithm an archive and a tuple of emitters MAPElites, CMAME\n\nEach abstract type documents a short list of required and optional functions, its contract. All generic code is written against these contracts, so any component that meets its contract works with all the others. See Extending the package.\n\nThe interface functions, such as evaluate, add! and vary, are public but not exported, because common names like evaluate clash with other packages. Call and extend them with the module name:\n\nQualityDiversity.evaluate(prob, x)\nQualityDiversity.evaluate(p::MyProblem, x) = ...\n\nConstructors, the solving functions and the metrics are exported.","title":"The five roles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Problems","page":"Concepts","text":"The quickest way to define a problem is to wrap a function that returns the fitness and the descriptor:\n\nusing QualityDiversity\nusing Random: Xoshiro\n\nf(x) = (-sum(abs2, x), clamp.((x[1], x[2]), -2, 2))      # fitness, descriptor\nprob = QDProblem(f; init=rng -> randn(rng, 5), bounds=((-2, 2), (-2, 2)))\nQualityDiversity.evaluate(prob, [0.5, -1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0])\nEvaluation{Float64, StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, Float64}, Nothing}(-1.25, [0.5, -1.0], nothing)\n\nFitness is maximized. To minimize a cost, return its negation.\ninit draws one random solution from the generator it is given; the archive is filled with such solutions before the first round.\nbounds gives the range of each descriptor dimension, which grid-like archives divide into cells. A problem whose descriptors take discrete values gives categories instead, such as (0:5, [:small, :large]), and gets a CategoricalArchive.\nThe function may also return a third value, the phenotype: the object that the genotype produces, when it differs from the genotype. Archives store it next to the solution when they are built with phenotype= its type.\n\nA problem with randomness in its evaluation is a StochasticQDProblem; see the noisy problems tutorial. A problem that needs its own type, for example to evaluate a whole batch at once, subtypes AbstractQDProblem.","title":"Problems"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#A-run","page":"Concepts","text":"An algorithm is an archive and a tuple of emitters. One round of a run is the same for every algorithm:\n\nfor emitter in emitters\n    xs  = ask(rng, emitter, archive)         # a batch of candidates\n    evs = evaluate_batch(rng, problem, xs)   # their fitness and descriptors\n    rs  = add_batch!(archive, xs, evs)       # what the archive did with each\n    tell!(emitter, xs, evs, rs)              # the emitter learns from it\nend\n\nDifferent algorithms are different archives and emitters, not different loops. The functions of CommonSolve.jl run it:\n\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(10, 10), batch_size=50)\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=20_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nQDResult after 20000 evaluations\n  archive: GridArchive with 91 of 100 cells occupied\n  qd_score: -168.19469254103396\n  coverage: 0.91\n  max_fitness: -0.002722293013079136\n  mean_fitness: -1.8482933246267468\n\nsolve is init followed by solve!. To look at the state between rounds, or to stop on a condition of your own, step by hand:\n\nstate = init(prob, alg; budget=20_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nwhile state.n_evals < 20_000 && coverage(state.archive) < 0.9\n    step!(state)\nend\nstate.n_evals, coverage(state.archive)\n(3750, 0.9)\n\ninit copies the archive and emitters of alg, so the same algorithm can be solved several times. The budget counts evaluations; a run stops at the end of the round that reaches it. callback(state) is called after initialization and after every round.","title":"A run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Results","page":"Concepts","text":"solve returns a QDResult with three fields:\n\narchive: the archive of the best solutions found;\nhistory: the metrics after initialization and after every round;\nn_evals: the number of evaluations spent.\n\nresult.history[end]\n(n_evals = 20000, qd_score = -168.19469254103396, coverage = 0.91, max_fitness = -0.002722293013079136, mean_fitness = -1.8482933246267468)\n\nelites iterates over the stored solutions and their evaluations:\n\nbest_x, best_ev = argmax(e -> e[2].fitness, collect(QualityDiversity.elites(result.archive)))\nbest_ev\nEvaluation{Float64, StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, Float64}, Nothing}(-0.002722293013079136, [0.024911987688377144, -0.036986867330716516], nothing)","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Metrics","page":"Concepts","text":"Metric Meaning\ncoverage fraction of the cells that hold a solution\nmax_fitness fitness of the best elite\nmean_fitness mean fitness of the elites\nqd_score sum of the fitness of the elites, each raised by offset\ncqd_score continuous QD score, which does not depend on the cells\n\nThe QD score sums fitness over the cells, so a negative fitness would lower it when a cell is filled. qd_score(archive; offset) adds offset to every elite; it should make every attainable fitness non-negative. A problem can supply its offset with fitness_offset, which the history uses.\n\nArchives with several objectives have metrics of their own, such as the MOQD score; see ParetoGridArchive.","title":"Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Randomness-and-threads","page":"Concepts","text":"Every function that draws random numbers takes the generator as its first argument, and a run takes its generator as rng. A run with a seeded generator is reproducible:\n\na = solve(prob, alg; budget=5_000, rng=Xoshiro(7))\nb = solve(prob, alg; budget=5_000, rng=Xoshiro(7))\na.history == b.history\ntrue\n\nThe default evaluate_batch evaluates the solutions of a batch on several threads (start Julia with --threads=auto to use them), so evaluate must be thread-safe. The results do not depend on the number of threads: a stochastic problem draws one seed per solution from the run's generator before the threaded section, in the order of the batch. Evaluation is the only part that runs on several threads; the archive and the emitters run on one.","title":"Randomness and threads"},{"category":"module","location":"guide/concepts/#QualityDiversity.QualityDiversity","page":"Concepts","text":"QualityDiversity\n\nQuality-diversity optimization for any genotype that can be written as a Julia type.\n\nFive roles make up an algorithm: a problem (AbstractQDProblem), an archive (AbstractArchive), variation operators (AbstractVariation), emitters (AbstractEmitter), and the algorithm that combines them (QDAlgorithm). Each role is an abstract type with a documented set of required and optional functions.\n\nInterface functions such as evaluate and add! are public but not exported. Extend them with qualified names:\n\nQualityDiversity.evaluate(p::MyProblem, x) = ...\n\nThe values of keyword arguments, such as the rankers and restart rules of a CMAEmitter, are public too: ranker=QualityDiversity.ObjectiveRanker(). The benchmark problems are in QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.QualityDiversity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#Metrics","page":"Metrics","text":"","title":"Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#Several-objectives","page":"Metrics","text":"","title":"Several objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrics/#Plotting","page":"Metrics","text":"Loading Makie (for example using CairoMakie) adds plot recipes for archives; see Plotting archives.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.qd_score","page":"Metrics","text":"qd_score(archive; offset=0)\n\nSum of fitness + offset over the occupied cells of archive. The offset keeps negative fitness values from lowering the score; use fitness_offset of the problem as a starting point.\n\nThe method for any AbstractArchive makes one pass over elites. An archive that keeps running totals specializes it, as GridArchive does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.qd_score"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.coverage","page":"Metrics","text":"coverage(archive)\n\nFraction of the cells of archive that are occupied, in [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.coverage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.max_fitness","page":"Metrics","text":"max_fitness(archive)\n\nHighest fitness among the occupied cells of archive. Throws an ArgumentError if the archive is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.max_fitness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.mean_fitness","page":"Metrics","text":"mean_fitness(archive)\n\nMean fitness over the occupied cells of archive. Throws an ArgumentError if the archive is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.mean_fitness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.cqd_score","page":"Metrics","text":"cqd_score(archive; targets, fitness_range, penalties=10, distance_max) -> CQDScore\ncqd_score(rng::AbstractRNG, archive; num_targets, fitness_range, penalties=10,\n          distance_max, bounds=descriptor_bounds(archive)) -> CQDScore\n\nThe continuous quality-diversity score of Kent, Branke, Gaier and Mouret (2022), \"A discretization-free metric for assessing quality diversity algorithms\"; pyribs cqd_score. It measures an archive without reference to its cells: a user asks for a solution near a target descriptor G and accepts a distance from it at a price θ, and the archive recommends the elite that maximizes\n\nω = f / (f_max - f_min) - θ * norm(d - G) / distance_max\n\nwhere f is the elite's fitness and d its descriptor. The score is the sum of the recommended ω over every target and every weight. As in the paper and in pyribs, the fitness is divided by the range, not shifted by f_min.\n\ntargets: descriptor points, each with the archive's number of dimensions. The second form draws num_targets of them uniformly within bounds with rng; the paper draws new targets for every run.\nfitness_range = (f_min, f_max): the bounds of the fitness, from the problem or from all the runs being compared; it has no default.\npenalties: the weights θ, or their number, spread evenly over [0, 1] with both ends included (a single weight is 0).\ndistance_max: the largest distance in descriptor space, by default the diagonal of descriptor_bounds(archive); an archive without bounds, such as an UnstructuredArchive, needs it given.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for an empty archive, a target of the wrong dimension or not finite, or invalid ranges. The time is proportional to the number of elites times the number of targets times the number of weights. To follow the score during a run, compute it in the callback of solve.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, StaticArrays\n\njulia> archive = GridArchive((2,), ((0, 1),));\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.add!(archive, [0.0], Evaluation(1.0, SVector(0.25)));\n\njulia> r = cqd_score(archive; targets=[[0.25], [0.75]], fitness_range=(0, 2), penalties=[0, 1]);\n\njulia> r.score, r.per_penalty\n(1.5, [0.5, 0.25])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.cqd_score"},{"category":"type","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.CQDScore","page":"Metrics","text":"CQDScore\n\nThe result of cqd_score: score, the continuous QD score; penalties, the penalty weights; per_penalty, the mean of ω over the targets for each weight, in the order of penalties; and num_targets. score is num_targets * sum(per_penalty). With Makie loaded, lines(result) plots per_penalty against penalties.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CQDScore"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.hypervolume","page":"Metrics","text":"hypervolume(points, reference_point) -> Float64\n\nHypervolume of a set of points, vectors of objective values that are maximized: the volume of the region that the points dominate and that lies above reference_point. A point contributes only if it is larger than the reference point in every objective, and a dominated point adds nothing. The empty set has volume 0.\n\nFor two objectives it sorts the points and adds the areas of the steps under them. For more it slices along the last objective and takes the hypervolume of each slice, which takes time of the order of n^(m-1) log n for n points and m objectives and suits the small sets that a Pareto front in a cell has.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity\n\njulia> hypervolume([[3.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0], [1.0, 3.0]], [0.0, 0.0])\n6.0\n\njulia> hypervolume([[1.0, 1.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0, 0.5]], [0.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n2.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.hypervolume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.max_hypervolume","page":"Metrics","text":"max_hypervolume(archive::ParetoGridArchive)\n\nLargest hypervolume of the front of one cell. Throws an ArgumentError for an empty archive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.max_hypervolume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.global_hypervolume","page":"Metrics","text":"global_hypervolume(archive::ParetoGridArchive)\n\nHypervolume of the Pareto front of all the solutions of the archive, from every cell, computed with hypervolume: for n solutions, num_solutions, and M objectives it takes time of the order of n log n for two objectives and n^(M-1) log n for more. Zero for an empty archive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.global_hypervolume"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversity.max_scores","page":"Metrics","text":"max_scores(archive::ParetoGridArchive) -> SVector\n\nHighest value of every objective over all the solutions of the archive. Throws an ArgumentError for an empty archive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.max_scores"},{"category":"module","location":"api/metrics/#QualityDiversityMakieExt","page":"Metrics","text":"QualityDiversityMakieExt\n\nMakie support for archives. heatmap(archive), heatmap!(ax, archive) and plot(archive) draw an archive with two descriptor dimensions as a heatmap of the elite fitness, with the descriptor dimensions on the axes and the empty cells left transparent.\n\nGridArchive: one rectangle per cell.\nSlidingBoundariesArchive: one rectangle per cell, between the current boundaries.\nCVTArchive: the Voronoi cells, sampled on a pixels by pixels grid (200 by default; pass pixels=...).\nCategoricalArchive: one square per combination of categories, on categorical axes that are labelled with the category values. Makie sorts the values of each axis, so they need isless.\nParetoGridArchive: the hypervolume of the front of each cell.\nThresholdArchive: the result archive, as solve returns it.\nSpreadArchive: the archive it wraps.\nDensityArchive: its result archive.\n\nscatter(archive), scatter!(ax, archive) and plot(archive) draw an UnstructuredArchive with two descriptor dimensions as one point per stored solution, coloured by its fitness, so that Colorbar(fig[1, 2], plot) shows the scale. The plot holds the scatter as its one child and takes the attributes of scatter, such as markersize.\n\necdfplot(archive) draws the empirical distribution function of the elite fitness of any archive with a single real fitness, as pyribs's archive_ecdf: the fraction of elites with fitness at most each value. An archive that wraps a result archive, such as a ThresholdArchive, shows its result archive.\n\nlines(result) for a CQDScore plots the mean of ω over the targets against the penalty weight, the figure of Kent et al. (2022).\n\nAn archive with another number of descriptor dimensions throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversityMakieExt"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Problems","page":"Problems","text":"A problem scores a solution and describes it. Most users wrap a function with QDProblem or StochasticQDProblem; subtyping AbstractQDProblem is for problems that need their own type, for example to define evaluate_batch.","title":"Problems"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Interface","page":"Problems","text":"","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Gradients","page":"Problems","text":"The algorithms of differentiable quality diversity (CMAMEGA, CMAMAEGA, OMGMEGA) need the gradients of the fitness and the descriptor. Loading ForwardDiff.jl computes them for most problems; a problem can also give them itself.","title":"Gradients"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Wrappers","page":"Problems","text":"","title":"Wrappers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Benchmarks","page":"Problems","text":"The benchmark problems live in the submodule QualityDiversity.Benchmarks, which exports them; load it with using QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.AbstractQDProblem","page":"Problems","text":"AbstractQDProblem\n\nSupertype of quality-diversity problems. A problem says how to score a solution and what a random solution looks like.\n\nFitness is maximized. A problem that minimizes returns the negated value.\n\nRequired methods\n\nevaluate(prob, x) → Evaluation\nrandom_solution(rng, prob)\ndescriptor_bounds(prob), or descriptor_categories(prob) for a problem whose descriptors take discrete values\n\nOptional methods\n\nevaluate_batch(prob, xs)\nfitness_offset(prob)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractQDProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.AbstractStochasticQDProblem","page":"Problems","text":"AbstractStochasticQDProblem\n\nSupertype of problems whose evaluation is random: scoring the same solution twice can give different fitness or descriptors. It is a subtype of AbstractQDProblem. Instead of evaluate(prob, x), such a problem defines\n\nevaluate(rng, prob, x) → Evaluation, which draws all its randomness from rng,\n\nnext to the other required methods of AbstractQDProblem.\n\nA run stays reproducible from its seed, whatever the number of threads. Before a batch is evaluated, evaluate_batch(rng, prob, xs) draws one seed per solution from the generator of the run, in the order of the batch, and gives every solution its own Xoshiro generator, so which thread evaluates a solution makes no difference. Deterministic problems do not use the generator of the run.\n\nStochasticQDProblem wraps a function, and Sampled evaluates a stochastic problem several times and returns the average.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AbstractStochasticQDProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Evaluation","page":"Problems","text":"Evaluation(fitness, descriptor)\nEvaluation(fitness, descriptor, phenotype)\nEvaluation{F,D,P}(fitness, descriptor, phenotype)\n\nThe result of evaluating one solution.\n\nfitness is a Real for a single objective or an AbstractVector for several. Fitness is maximized; a problem that minimizes returns the negated value. Archives take only finite fitness values: a problem that wants to mark a solution as infeasible returns a low finite value. descriptor locates the solution in descriptor space and is usually an SVector{N,Float64}.\n\nphenotype is what the problem computed from the genotype and described, when the two differ: the product of a reaction sequence, the shape that a set of assembly rules builds. An archive whose phenotype type is P stores it next to the genotype, and elites returns it, so it need not be computed again. Variation sees only genotypes. Without a phenotype the field is nothing and P is Nothing, which takes no memory in an archive.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, StaticArrays\n\njulia> Evaluation(1.0, SVector(0.5, 0.5)).phenotype === nothing\ntrue\n\njulia> Evaluation(1.0, SVector(0.5, 0.5), \"CCO\").phenotype\n\"CCO\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Evaluation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.evaluate","page":"Problems","text":"evaluate(prob::AbstractQDProblem, x) -> Evaluation\nevaluate(rng::AbstractRNG, prob::AbstractStochasticQDProblem, x) -> Evaluation\n\nFitness and descriptor of one solution x, and its phenotype if the problem computes one; see Evaluation. Required. A stochastic problem takes the generator that it draws its randomness from as the first argument; see AbstractStochasticQDProblem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.evaluate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.evaluate_batch","page":"Problems","text":"evaluate_batch(prob::AbstractQDProblem, xs) -> Vector{<:Evaluation}\nevaluate_batch(rng::AbstractRNG, prob::AbstractQDProblem, xs) -> Vector{<:Evaluation}\n\nEvaluate every solution in xs, returning one Evaluation per solution in the same order. Optional: a problem specializes it to evaluate a batch at once, for example on a GPU or through a call into Python.\n\nThe default calls evaluate on each solution, on several threads when the batch is worth it, so evaluate must be thread-safe. It times the first evaluation of the batch and runs the rest in parallel only if the whole batch would take longer than about 100 µs on one thread: starting the threads costs 10 to 30 µs, more than a batch of a cheap function. The results do not depend on the number of threads. An error that evaluate throws comes out as it is, not wrapped in the exception of a task. The element type of the vector is inferred from evaluate, so a problem whose fitness or descriptor type varies from solution to solution gets a Union element type.\n\nTo evaluate on a GPU, a problem type defines this method: it copies the batch to the device, runs array operations or a kernel, and copies the fitness and descriptor values back. examples/gpu_evaluation.jl does this for any array type of the GPU packages (CUDA.jl, Metal.jl, AMDGPU.jl, oneAPI.jl). The package itself does not depend on any of them.\n\nThe candidates of a DeviceCellArchive are a DeviceBatch, whose solutions are the columns of a device matrix. For them the default runs evaluate(prob, x) in a kernel, one solution per thread (for a QDProblem, its function), and returns an EvaluationBatch on the device. That code must then compile for the device: no allocation, no error message built from values, and no Float64 on a device that lacks it. A problem can instead define evaluate_batch(prob::MyProblem, xs::DeviceBatch) and work on parent(xs).\n\nThe method with a generator is the one that step! calls, with the generator of the run. For a deterministic problem it calls evaluate_batch(prob, xs) and leaves the generator untouched. For an AbstractStochasticQDProblem it draws one seed per solution from rng, in the order of the batch, and evaluates solution i with evaluate(Xoshiro(seed_i), prob, xs[i]), so the results do not depend on the number of threads. A stochastic problem can define its own method with a generator, for example to evaluate a batch on a device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.evaluate_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.random_solution","page":"Problems","text":"random_solution(rng::AbstractRNG, prob::AbstractQDProblem)\n\nOne random genotype, used to initialize the archive. Required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.random_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.default_genotype","page":"Problems","text":"default_genotype(prob::AbstractQDProblem) -> Type\n\nThe genotype type that the archives and algorithms built from prob store when genotype is not given: the type of random_solution(Xoshiro(0), prob). Optional; a problem whose random solutions do not all have the type the archive should hold defines it, or passes genotype to the constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.default_genotype"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.descriptor_bounds","page":"Problems","text":"descriptor_bounds(prob::AbstractQDProblem)\n\nPer-dimension (lo, hi) bounds of the descriptor space, as a tuple of tuples. Archives use them to lay out their cells. Required, unless the problem defines descriptor_categories; the fallback returns nothing.\n\ndescriptor_bounds(archive::AbstractArchive)\n\nThe same for an archive that covers a box of descriptor space, as GridArchive, CVTArchive and ThresholdArchive do. Optional; the fallback returns nothing, for an archive that covers no box. The DirectionRankers need it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.descriptor_bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.descriptor_categories","page":"Problems","text":"descriptor_categories(prob::AbstractQDProblem)\n\nPer-dimension collections of the values a discrete descriptor can take, as a tuple, for example (0:5, [:alkane, :aromatic, :ring]). A problem that defines it returns descriptors as tuples with one entry from each collection, such as (3, :ring), and is used with a CategoricalArchive. Optional: a problem with real-valued descriptors defines descriptor_bounds instead, and the fallback returns nothing, which marks such a problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.descriptor_categories"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.fitness_offset","page":"Problems","text":"fitness_offset(prob::AbstractQDProblem)\n\nOffset that makes every attainable fitness non-negative, as the default offset of qd_score. Optional; the default is 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.fitness_offset"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.reference_point","page":"Problems","text":"reference_point(prob::AbstractQDProblem)\n\nA point that lies below the fitness values of interest of a problem with several objectives, as the corner from which a ParetoGridArchive measures hypervolume. Optional: MOME takes it from the problem unless it is given.\n\nreference_point(archive::ParetoGridArchive)\n\nThe reference point of the archive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.reference_point"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.evaluations_per_solution","page":"Problems","text":"evaluations_per_solution(prob::AbstractQDProblem) -> Int\n\nHow many times the underlying function is evaluated to score one solution. Optional; the default is 1. Sampled returns its number of samples, and the budget of a run counts these evaluations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.evaluations_per_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.evaluate_gradient","page":"Problems","text":"evaluate_gradient(prob::AbstractQDProblem, x) -> (Evaluation, jacobian)\n\nThe Evaluation of the solution x together with the gradients of its fitness and descriptor, for the algorithms of differentiable quality diversity (CMAMEGA, CMAMAEGA, OMGMEGA). Optional: only those algorithms call it.\n\nx is a real vector of length n and the descriptor has N dimensions. jacobian is an (N + 1) × n matrix whose first row is the gradient of the fitness and whose row j + 1 is the gradient of descriptor component j. The fitness is a single real number.\n\nThe default differentiates evaluate(prob, x) with ForwardDiff.jl, which must be loaded (using ForwardDiff). The code of the problem must then compute in the number type of x rather than convert its entries to Float64, since ForwardDiff passes vectors of dual numbers; a QDProblem does, if its function does. A problem defines this method itself to give its gradients in closed form, which is faster, or to compute them with another automatic-differentiation package. Sphere and Rastrigin define it in closed form. Stochastic problems have no gradient.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, ForwardDiff\n\njulia> prob = QDProblem(x -> (-sum(abs2, x), (x[1], 2x[2]));\n                        init = rng -> randn(rng, 2), bounds = ((-3, 3), (-6, 6)));\n\njulia> ev, jacobian = QualityDiversity.evaluate_gradient(prob, [1.0, 2.0]);\n\njulia> ev.fitness\n-5.0\n\njulia> jacobian\n3×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n -2.0  -4.0\n  1.0   0.0\n  0.0   2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.evaluate_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.evaluate_gradient_batch","page":"Problems","text":"evaluate_gradient_batch(prob::AbstractQDProblem, xs) -> (evaluations, jacobians)\n\nevaluate_gradient for every solution in xs: a vector of Evaluations and a vector of Jacobian matrices, in the order of xs. Optional: the default calls evaluate_gradient on each solution in parallel with Threads.@threads, so it must be thread-safe. A problem specializes it to differentiate a batch at once. Every solution counts as one evaluation against the budget of a run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.evaluate_gradient_batch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.QDProblem","page":"Problems","text":"QDProblem(f; init, bounds)\nQDProblem(f; init, categories)\n\nWrap a plain function as a problem. f(x) returns a (fitness, descriptor) tuple for the solution x, or a (fitness, descriptor, phenotype) tuple for a problem whose phenotype an archive should store; see Evaluation. init(rng) returns one random solution.\n\nWith bounds, a tuple with one (lo, hi) pair per descriptor dimension, the descriptor is real-valued and evaluate returns it as an SVector{N,Float64}, or with the number type of its components when that is wider, so that ForwardDiff can differentiate evaluate. With categories, a tuple with one collection of allowed values per dimension, for example (0:5, [:alkane, :aromatic, :ring]), the descriptor is discrete and evaluate returns it as a tuple, which a CategoricalArchive takes as it is.\n\nf is called from several threads at once by the default evaluate_batch, so it must be thread-safe.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> prob = QDProblem(x -> (-sum(abs2, x), (x[1], x[2]));\n                        init = rng -> randn(rng, 2), bounds = ((-3, 3), (-3, 3)));\n\njulia> ev = QualityDiversity.evaluate(prob, [1.0, 2.0]);\n\njulia> ev.fitness, ev.descriptor\n(-5.0, [1.0, 2.0])\n\njulia> labelled = QDProblem(x -> (-sum(abs2, x), (length(x), :ring));\n                            init = rng -> randn(rng, 2), categories = (1:3, [:chain, :ring]));\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.evaluate(labelled, [1.0, 2.0]).descriptor\n(2, :ring)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.QDProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.StochasticQDProblem","page":"Problems","text":"StochasticQDProblem(f; init, bounds)\nStochasticQDProblem(f; init, categories)\n\nWrap a function of a random number generator and a solution as a stochastic problem. f(rng, x) returns a (fitness, descriptor) or (fitness, descriptor, phenotype) tuple for the solution x and draws every random number from rng. The other arguments are those of QDProblem. See AbstractStochasticQDProblem for how the generators are made, and note that f is called from several threads at once.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> prob = StochasticQDProblem((rng, x) -> (-sum(abs2, x) + 0.1 * randn(rng), (x[1], x[2]));\n                                  init = rng -> randn(rng, 2), bounds = ((-3, 3), (-3, 3)));\n\njulia> a = QualityDiversity.evaluate(Xoshiro(1), prob, [1.0, 2.0]);\n\njulia> a == QualityDiversity.evaluate(Xoshiro(1), prob, [1.0, 2.0])\ntrue\n\njulia> a.fitness == QualityDiversity.evaluate(Xoshiro(2), prob, [1.0, 2.0]).fitness\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.StochasticQDProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Sampled","page":"Problems","text":"Sampled(prob; num_samples, expectation=Average())\n\nScore every solution of the stochastic problem prob with num_samples evaluations and return the Expectation of their fitness and, for every dimension, of their descriptor. An archive then holds the averages and not the single lucky evaluation that a noisy problem can give a poor solution.\n\nSampled is itself a stochastic problem; the evaluations of one solution run one after the other with the generator of that solution, so the results are reproducible from the seed of the run. Every evaluation counts against the budget of a run, so num_samples times fewer solutions are scored for the same budget; see evaluations_per_solution. The fitness must be a real number and the descriptor a vector of real numbers. The descriptor bounds and the fitness offset are those of prob.\n\nIf prob returns a phenotype, the result has the phenotype that occurs most often among the evaluations, compared with isequal; on a tie, the one that occurs first. The expectation applies to the fitness and the descriptor only. A phenotype type without its own isequal and hash compares by identity, so every evaluation differs and the result has the phenotype of the first.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> noisy = StochasticQDProblem((rng, x) -> (-x[1]^2 + randn(rng), (x[1], x[2]));\n                                   init = rng -> randn(rng, 2), bounds = ((-3, 3), (-3, 3)));\n\njulia> prob = Sampled(noisy; num_samples=100);\n\njulia> ev = QualityDiversity.evaluate(Xoshiro(1), prob, [1.0, 0.5]);\n\njulia> abs(ev.fitness + 1) < 0.5\ntrue\n\njulia> QualityDiversity.evaluations_per_solution(prob)\n100\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Sampled"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.SampledSpread","page":"Problems","text":"SampledSpread(prob; num_samples, expectation=Average())\n\nScore every solution of the stochastic problem prob with num_samples evaluations, as Sampled does, but keep every descriptor: the evaluation has the Expectation of the fitness, the descriptors of all the evaluations as a DescriptorSamples, and the phenotype that occurs most often. A SpreadArchive, the archive of MELS, takes these evaluations, and only it: the cell of a solution and its spread depend on the cells of the archive.\n\nThe evaluations of one solution run one after the other with the generator of that solution, and each counts against the budget of a run. The fitness must be a real number and the descriptor a vector of real numbers. The descriptor bounds and the fitness offset are those of prob.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using QualityDiversity, Random\n\njulia> noisy = StochasticQDProblem((rng, x) -> (-x[1]^2 + randn(rng), (x[1], x[2] + 0.1 * randn(rng)));\n                                   init = rng -> randn(rng, 2), bounds = ((-3, 3), (-3, 3)));\n\njulia> ev = QualityDiversity.evaluate(Xoshiro(1), SampledSpread(noisy; num_samples=5), [1.0, 0.5]);\n\njulia> length(ev.descriptor), ev.descriptor[1][1]\n(5, 1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.SampledSpread"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.DescriptorSamples","page":"Problems","text":"DescriptorSamples{N}(samples::Vector{SVector{N,Float64}})\n\nThe descriptors of the evaluations of one solution, in the order of the evaluations: the descriptor of an Evaluation of a SampledSpread problem. It is an AbstractVector whose entries are the descriptors. A SpreadArchive finds the cell and the spread of the solution from them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DescriptorSamples"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Expectation","page":"Problems","text":"Expectation\n\nSupertype of the rules that turn the results of several evaluations of one solution into one: Average and Median. A rule defines expectation(rule, values) for a vector of real numbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Expectation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.expectation","page":"Problems","text":"expectation(rule::Expectation, values::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe number that rule makes of the evaluations values of one solution. A new rule defines this method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.expectation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Average","page":"Problems","text":"Average()\n\nThe mean of the evaluations. This is the default of Sampled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Average"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Median","page":"Problems","text":"Median()\n\nThe median of the evaluations, which an outlier moves less than it moves the mean. It is taken for every descriptor dimension separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Median"},{"category":"module","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Benchmarks","page":"Problems","text":"QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\n\nThe benchmark problems of the package: Rastrigin, Sphere and BiSphere. They are not exported from QualityDiversity, because Sphere is also a name of Makie and GeometryBasics; load them with\n\nusing QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nprob = Rastrigin(10)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Benchmarks"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin","page":"Problems","text":"Rastrigin(dim)\n\nThe Rastrigin function as a quality-diversity benchmark over dim real parameters, dim ≥ 2.\n\nThe solution is clipped to [-5.12, 5.12] before it is scored. Fitness is the negated Rastrigin value, so the maximum is 0 at the origin. The descriptor is the mean of the first dim ÷ 2 clipped parameters and the mean of the remaining ones, which both lie in [-5.12, 5.12].\n\nSolutions are Vector{Float64}, drawn uniformly from the clipping box.\n\nevaluate computes in Float64, or in the element type of the solution when that carries more. On a device batch the kernel computes in the element type of the solution, so Float32 solutions are scored in Float32 there, and their fitness can differ from that of evaluate in the last digits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Sphere","page":"Problems","text":"Sphere(dim)\n\nThe sphere with a linear projection of Fontaine et al. (2020), a quality-diversity benchmark over dim real parameters, dim ≥ 2. Its objective is the negated sphere function centered at 0.4 * 5.12 in every parameter, scaled so that the optimum has fitness 100 and the corner -5.12 of the box has fitness 0; a solution farther away has a negative fitness.\n\nThe descriptor is the sum of the first dim ÷ 2 parameters and the sum of the rest, after each parameter outside [-5.12, 5.12] is replaced by 5.12 / θ. Because that sum projects many parameters onto one number, most of the solutions land near the center of the descriptor space, and reaching its edges takes solutions that are all at the same end of the box. The descriptor lies in [-5.12 * dim/2, 5.12 * dim/2] in both dimensions.\n\nSolutions are Vector{Float64}, drawn uniformly from [-5.12, 5.12].\n\nevaluate computes in Float64, or in the element type of the solution when that carries more. On a device batch the kernel computes in the element type of the solution, so Float32 solutions are scored in Float32 there, and their fitness can differ from that of evaluate in the last digits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Sphere"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.BiSphere","page":"Problems","text":"BiSphere(dim)\n\nA benchmark with two objectives over dim real parameters, dim ≥ 2: the negated squared distances to the two points (1, …, 1) and (-1, …, -1). No solution is best in both; the solutions that trade them off best lie on the line between the two points.\n\nThe solution is clipped to [-5.12, 5.12] before it is scored. The fitness is an SVector{2,Float64} of the two objectives, which are at most 0. The descriptor is that of Rastrigin: the mean of the first dim ÷ 2 clipped parameters and the mean of the remaining ones. reference_point is the lowest value of both objectives, so every solution adds hypervolume.\n\nSolutions are Vector{Float64}, drawn uniformly from the clipping box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.BiSphere"},{"category":"section","location":"api/device/#Device-archives-and-emitters","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"These types run MAP-Elites and CMA-ME with the archive in the arrays of a GPU; see the GPU guide. Their kernels need KernelAbstractions.jl, which the GPU packages load.","title":"Device archives and emitters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/device/#Archives","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"","title":"Archives"},{"category":"section","location":"api/device/#Batches","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"","title":"Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"api/device/#Emitters","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"","title":"Emitters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.DeviceCellArchive","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"DeviceCellArchive{T,F,N}\n\nSupertype of the archives that keep their cells in the arrays of a device, for MAP-Elites on a GPU: DeviceGridArchive and DeviceCVTArchive. They have the cells and the rules of their host counterparts, GridArchive and CVTArchive, and differ from each other only in how a descriptor is assigned to a cell. They store solutions that are real vectors of a fixed length with entries of a floating-point type T, fitness values of type F, and descriptors with N components, and no phenotypes.\n\nThe device is chosen by the array type that the archive is built with: CuArray (CUDA.jl), ROCArray (AMDGPU.jl), oneArray (oneAPI.jl), or Array, which runs the same kernels on the CPU. The kernels that insert solutions and that vary them in a DeviceVariationEmitter are written with KernelAbstractions.jl, which every GPU package above loads; with Array load it yourself (using KernelAbstractions). The cell of a descriptor is computed in Float64, as on the host, and descriptors are stored and passed between kernels in the element type of the descriptor type, Float64, whatever T is, so that both assign the same cells; a device without Float64, such as an Apple GPU, cannot run it.\n\nStorage is one array per field, on the device: the genotypes as a dim × cells matrix, the descriptors as an N × cells matrix of Float64, the fitness values, which cells are occupied, and the occupied cells in increasing order, from which parents are drawn uniformly.\n\nadd_batch! follows the rule of the host cell archives: every result is computed against the archive as it was before the batch, and of the solutions that enter a cell the best is kept, the first in the batch on a tie. A fitness value that is NaN or infinite throws an ArgumentError, as does a descriptor outside the bounds with ThrowOutside() or a NaN component with ClampOutside(), before the archive changes.\n\nThe other functions of the archive contract work on the host: add! adds one solution through add_batch!, and elites and sample_elites copy the archive to the host first. The metrics are reductions on the device. Consumer GPUs compute Float64 many times more slowly than Float32: use genotype=Vector{Float32} and fitness=Float32 there.\n\nA subtype has these fields, which the shared methods and kernels use: lower and upper (SVector{N,Float64}), outside (an OutsidePolicy), and the device arrays that device_storage allocates, with count, the number of occupied cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DeviceCellArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.DeviceGridArchive","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"DeviceGridArchive{T,F}(device, dims, bounds, dim; outside=ThrowOutside())\nDeviceGridArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; dims, device, bounds=nothing,\n                  genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, outside=ThrowOutside())\n\nA GridArchive held in the arrays of a device, for MAP-Elites on a GPU: the same cells and rules, for solutions that are real vectors of length dim with entries of a floating-point type T, and fitness values of type F. device is the array type to store them in, such as CuArray; see DeviceCellArchive for the storage, the kernels and what runs on the host. The second form takes T from genotype, F from fitness and dim from random_solution(rng, prob).\n\nMAPElites with device builds this archive with a DeviceVariationEmitter. GridArchive(archive) returns a host copy, for example to plot it.\n\nExample\n\nusing CUDA\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(20)\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(100, 100), batch_size=10_000, device=CuArray,\n                genotype=Vector{Float32}, fitness=Float32, variation=GaussianMutation(0.5f0))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=1_000_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\ncoverage(result.archive), GridArchive(result.archive)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DeviceGridArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.DeviceCVTArchive","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"DeviceCVTArchive{T,F}(device, centroids, bounds, dim; outside=ThrowOutside())\nDeviceCVTArchive{T,F}(device, cells, bounds, dim; rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                      samples=100_000, max_iterations=100, outside=ThrowOutside())\nDeviceCVTArchive(prob::AbstractQDProblem; cells, device, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n                 bounds=nothing, samples=100_000, max_iterations=100,\n                 genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, outside=ThrowOutside())\n\nA CVTArchive held in the arrays of a device, for MAP-Elites on a GPU: the Voronoi cells of a set of centroids, with the rules of the host archive, for solutions that are real vectors of length dim with entries of a floating-point type T, and fitness values of type F. device is the array type to store them in, such as CuArray; see DeviceCellArchive for the storage, the kernels and what runs on the host.\n\nThe centroids are given, or chosen on the host by k-means from samples uniform points of the box bounds, exactly as for a CVTArchive: the same centroids, or the same generator and seed, give the same cells, and centroids(archive) returns them. For a reproducible run, seed the archive as well as solve, e.g. rng=Xoshiro(1): without it, rng is Random.default_rng(), which the seed of solve does not set. The last form takes T from genotype, F from fitness and dim from random_solution(rng, prob).\n\nOn the device, the box is divided into a regular grid of about as many buckets as cells, and each bucket lists the centroids that can be nearest to a point in it, computed once on the host. A kernel finds the bucket of a descriptor and compares it with those centroids only, one thread per solution. The distances are computed in Float64 in the order of the host archive's k-d tree, so the two assign the same cell, the lowest index if two centroids are equally near. In many descriptor dimensions the buckets hold more centroids, and in the limit one bucket holds all of them.\n\nMAPElites(archive) runs MAP-Elites over it with a DeviceVariationEmitter. CVTArchive(archive) returns a host copy, for example to plot it.\n\nExample\n\nusing CUDA\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(20)\narchive = DeviceCVTArchive(prob; cells=10_000, device=CuArray, rng=Xoshiro(1),\n                           genotype=Vector{Float32}, fitness=Float32)\nalg = MAPElites(archive; batch_size=10_000, variation=GaussianMutation(0.5f0))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=1_000_000, rng=Xoshiro(2))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DeviceCVTArchive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.DeviceBatch","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"DeviceBatch(X::AbstractMatrix)\n\nA batch of real-vector solutions held as the columns of the matrix X, which may be a device array. It is an AbstractVector whose jth element is view(X, :, j); parent(batch) returns X. A DeviceVariationEmitter returns its candidates as a DeviceBatch, and evaluate_batch and add_batch! of a DeviceCellArchive take it without copying it to the host. A problem that scores a batch with array operations defines evaluate_batch(prob, xs::DeviceBatch) and works on parent(xs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DeviceBatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.EvaluationBatch","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"EvaluationBatch{N}(fitness::AbstractVector, descriptors::AbstractMatrix)\n\nThe evaluations of a batch of solutions as two arrays, which may be device arrays: the fitness of solution j is fitness[j] and its descriptor the column descriptors[:, j], with N rows. It is an AbstractVector of Evaluations with SVector{N,Float64} descriptors and no phenotype; reading one element copies it to the host. The default evaluate_batch of a DeviceBatch returns one, and add_batch! of a DeviceCellArchive reads its arrays where they are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.EvaluationBatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.AddResultBatch","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"AddResultBatch{F}\n\nThe results of add_batch! on a DeviceCellArchive for a DeviceBatch: an AbstractVector of AddResults whose fields are kept in two arrays on the device, status (the AddStatus as a UInt8) and improvement. Reading one element copies it to the host; collect copies all of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.AddResultBatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.DeviceVariationEmitter","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"DeviceVariationEmitter(op; batch_size)\n\nThe emitter of MAP-Elites over a DeviceCellArchive: it draws parents uniformly from the occupied cells and applies the variation operator op, giving batch_size children, all on the device. The children come back as a DeviceBatch, which evaluate_batch and add_batch! take without copying it to the host. A later ask returns a new batch, so a batch stays valid after the next one is drawn.\n\nop is a GaussianMutation or an IsoLineVariation; its standard deviations are converted to the element type of the archive. Each ask draws one UInt64 from the run's generator, and the kernels derive their random numbers from it with the counter-based generator Philox4x32-10, keyed on the child and the entry, so a run is reproducible from its seed on a given backend however the threads are scheduled. These random numbers differ from those of a VariationEmitter on the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DeviceVariationEmitter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/device/#QualityDiversity.DeviceCMAEmitter","page":"Device archives and emitters","text":"DeviceCMAEmitter(dim; num_emitters, sigma0, kwargs...)\n\nThe emitter of CMA-ME over a DeviceCellArchive: num_emitters independent CMA-ES searches, each a CMAEmitter with the keyword arguments of CMAEmitter, that ask together. Their candidates form one batch, which is evaluated and added to the archive on the device at once, so every search of a round sees the archive as it was before the round, as in pyribs. A host CMAME instead steps its emitters one after another, as in the CMA-ME paper. The searches that restart draw their new starts from the archive together, at the start of the round. A device only pays off with many searches, or many candidates per search: the batch has num_emitters times the batch size of one search.\n\nThe searches stay on the host: each draws its candidates from its own distribution, the searches in parallel on the threads of the host, each with a generator seeded by a number drawn in order from the run's generator, so that a run is reproducible from its seed whatever the number of threads. The batch is then copied to the device. After the batch is added, its fitness values, descriptors and results are copied back once, and each search ranks its part and adapts its distribution as a CMAEmitter does, the searches in parallel on the threads of the host.\n\nThe property searches holds the searches, for example to read their iterations and restarts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DeviceCMAEmitter"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/gpu/#Running-on-a-GPU","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"Everything runs on the CPU unless you ask for a device, and the package does not depend on any GPU package. There are two ways to use a GPU, depending on what is expensive.","title":"Running on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/gpu/#Evaluating-a-batch-on-a-GPU","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"When the evaluation is expensive and the rest of the run is cheap, keep the archive on the CPU and evaluate each batch on the GPU. The loop calls evaluate_batch once per round, and a problem type can define it: copy the batch to the device, compute with array operations or a kernel, and copy the fitness and descriptors back.\n\nfunction QualityDiversity.evaluate_batch(prob::MyProblem, xs::AbstractVector)\n    X = CuArray(reduce(hcat, xs))                     # one column per solution\n    fitness, descriptors = my_gpu_scoring(X)          # array code on the device\n    f, d = Array(fitness), Array(descriptors)\n    return [Evaluation(f[j], SVector(d[1, j], d[2, j])) for j in eachindex(f)]\nend\n\nexamples/gpu_evaluation.jl does this for the Rastrigin function with any array type (CuArray, ROCArray, MtlArray, oneArray), and is tested with JLArrays, an array type that runs on the CPU.","title":"Evaluating a batch on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/gpu/#MAP-Elites-and-CMA-ME-on-a-device","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"When evaluation is cheap, the time goes into the archive and the variation, and moving a batch to the GPU and back every round costs more than it saves. Then the whole run can stay on the device:\n\nusing CUDA, QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nusing Random: Xoshiro\n\nprob = Rastrigin(100)\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(100, 100), batch_size=10_000, device=CuArray,\n                genotype=Vector{Float32}, fitness=Float32)\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=10_000_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\n\nWith device, MAPElites builds a DeviceGridArchive, whose cells are in arrays of that type, and a DeviceVariationEmitter, which draws parents and mutates them in a kernel. CMAME accepts device too and builds a DeviceCMAEmitter, many CMA-ES searches whose candidates are evaluated and added together. For a CVT archive, build a DeviceCVTArchive and pass it to MAPElites(archive).\n\nKernels. The kernels are written with KernelAbstractions.jl, which CUDA.jl, AMDGPU.jl and oneAPI.jl load. device=Array runs the same kernels on the CPU after using KernelAbstractions, which is how the tests run them.\nEvaluation. By default the problem's evaluate runs in a kernel, one solution per thread, so it must compile for the device: no allocation and no error messages built from values. Rastrigin, Sphere and a QDProblem with a simple function work. Otherwise define evaluate_batch(prob, xs::DeviceBatch) and work on parent(xs), a matrix with one solution per column.\nPrecision. On consumer GPUs use Float32 solutions and fitness, and a problem that computes in Float32; see Float32 on a GPU.\nReproducibility. A run is reproducible from its seed on a given device, whatever the scheduling of the threads, but its random numbers differ from those of the CPU path.\nResults. GridArchive(archive) and CVTArchive(archive) copy a device archive to the CPU, for plotting and analysis.\n\nThe device archives support MAP-Elites with GaussianMutation and IsoLineVariation, and CMA-ME. Stochastic problems, phenotypes and the other archives run on the CPU.","title":"MAP-Elites and CMA-ME on a device"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/gpu/#Float32-on-a-GPU","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"Consumer GPUs compute Float64 many times more slowly than Float32, so on them a run should use Float32 wherever the precision is enough. Four things have a precision, and they are set in different places:\n\nWhat Type on a consumer GPU Set by\nsolutions Float32 genotype=Vector{Float32}\nfitness stored in the archive Float32 fitness=Float32\nthe arithmetic of the evaluation whatever the problem's code computes in the problem\ndescriptors, bounds and the cell lookup always Float64 the archive\n\nDescriptors stay Float64. A device archive and a CPU archive put every solution in the same cell. That needs the descriptor at full precision: a descriptor just below a cell boundary, such as 0.499999999 with a boundary at 0.5, rounds to 0.5f0 in Float32 and would land in the next cell. Descriptors are a few numbers per solution, so keeping them in Float64 costs little. A GPU without Float64, such as an Apple GPU, cannot run the device archives.\n\nThe arithmetic is set by the problem, not by genotype. With genotype=Vector{Float32} the problem receives Float32 solutions, but nothing makes its code compute in Float32. A single Float64 value, such as the literal 10.0 or 5.12, promotes the whole computation to Float64. The run is then correct but slow, and nothing reports it, since the archive converts the fitness to Float32 when it stores it.\n\nWrite the scoring function in the element type of the solution:\n\nusing QualityDiversity\n\nfunction rastrigin(x::AbstractVector{T}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n    limit = T(5.12)                          # 5.12 alone is a Float64\n    total = zero(T)\n    for xi in x\n        xi = clamp(xi, -limit, limit)\n        total += xi^2 - 10 * cospi(2 * xi)   # integers keep the type T\n    end\n    h = length(x) ÷ 2\n    first_half = sum(view(x, 1:h)) / h\n    second_half = sum(view(x, h+1:length(x))) / (length(x) - h)\n    return -(10 * length(x) + total), (first_half, second_half)\nend\n\nprob = QDProblem(rastrigin; init=rng -> 10 .* rand(rng, Float32, 20) .- 5,\n                 bounds=((-5.12, 5.12), (-5.12, 5.12)))\n\nIntegers, zero(T), one(T), T(...) and Float32 literals such as 5.12f0 keep the type; Float64 literals and Float64(...) do not.\n\nCheck the precision on the CPU before running on a GPU: one Float32 solution must give a Float32 fitness.\n\nx = rand(Float32, 20)\ntypeof(QualityDiversity.evaluate(prob, x).fitness)   # Float32; Float64 means the code promotes\n\nThe descriptor of that evaluation is an SVector{2,Float64} whatever the code computes in, since descriptors are always Float64.\n\nThe built-in Rastrigin and Sphere compute on a device in the element type of the solution, so the example at the top of this page evaluates in Float32. Their evaluate on the CPU computes in Float64, also with Float32 solutions, so the check above does not apply to them, and the same Float32 solution can get a fitness on the device that differs from the CPU one in the last digits.","title":"Float32 on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/gpu/#When-it-pays-off","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"A GPU pays off for large batches. On the benchmark of the comparison, MAP-Elites with batches of 100 or fewer is faster on one CPU core than on a GPU, and with batches of 10 000 the GPU is about 26 times faster. A device CMA-ME is slower than the CPU with the five searches of the default and faster with hundreds.","title":"When it pays off"},{"category":"section","location":"#QualityDiversity.jl","page":"Home","text":"Quality-diversity (QD) optimization in Julia: MAP-Elites and the algorithms built from the same parts, such as CMA-ME, CMA-MAE, MOME and Dominated Novelty Search.\n\nA QD algorithm does not look for one best solution. It looks for the best solution of every kind, where the kind of a solution is given by a few numbers called its descriptor, and returns an archive of them: the most efficient network for each density and clustering, the fastest gait for each way of using the legs.\n\nQualityDiversity.jl works with any genotype a user can write as a Julia type: vectors of numbers, and also graphs, molecules, programs or recipes. You define the type, a function that scores and describes it, and a way to vary it; the library compiles specialized code for it. Real vectors are supported throughout and get good defaults.","title":"QualityDiversity.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"The package is not yet registered. Install it from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/wclaerhout/QualityDiversity.jl\")\n\nIt needs Julia 1.10 or later.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nusing Random: Xoshiro\n\nprob = Rastrigin(10)                                  # a benchmark with 10 parameters\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20), variation=GaussianMutation(0.3))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\n\ncoverage(result.archive), max_fitness(result.archive)\n(0.7325, -25.312960374489037)\n\nFor a problem of your own, wrap a function that returns the fitness (maximized) and the descriptor:\n\nf(x) = (-sum(abs2, x), clamp.((x[1], x[2]), -2, 2))\nprob = QDProblem(f; init=rng -> randn(rng, 5), bounds=((-2, 2), (-2, 2)))\nresult = solve(prob, MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20)); budget=50_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nresult.history[end]\n(n_evals = 50048, qd_score = -949.6862010820092, coverage = 1.0, max_fitness = -0.0021033646287535518, mean_fitness = -2.374215502705023)\n\nSwitching algorithm is one argument:\n\nsolve(prob, CMAME(prob; dims=(20, 20), sigma0=0.5); budget=50_000)\n\nWith a Makie backend loaded, heatmap(result.archive) draws the archive (scatter for an UnstructuredArchive); with Tables.jl loaded, DataFrame(result.archive) lists its elites.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Exported-and-public-names","page":"Home","text":"using QualityDiversity brings in the names a script needs: the problem wrappers, the archives, variations, emitters and algorithms, solve and the metrics. The rest of the interface is public but not exported, so it never clashes with another package and you write it with a qualified name: the functions you extend for a type of your own, such as QualityDiversity.evaluate, and the values of keyword arguments, such as CMAME(prob; ranker=QualityDiversity.ObjectiveRanker(), ...). The benchmark problems live in the submodule QualityDiversity.Benchmarks, which exports them:\n\nusing QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nprob = Sphere(20)","title":"Exported and public names"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-is-included","page":"Home","text":"Algorithms: MAP-Elites, CMA-ME, CMA-MAE, CMA-MEGA, CMA-MAEGA and OMG-MEGA (differentiable quality diversity), Density Descent Search, MOME (several objectives), MAP-Elites with low spread (noisy problems), novelty-style archives and Dominated Novelty Search, and a bandit scheduler to choose among emitters.\nArchives: grid, CVT, sliding-boundaries, categorical, threshold, unstructured, dominated-novelty, density, spread, gradient and Pareto archives.\nEvolution strategies for the CMA emitters: CMA-ES, sep-CMA-ES, LM-MA-ES and OpenAI-ES, with the rankers, selection and restart rules of pyribs.\nNoisy problems, evaluated with a generator of their own so that runs stay reproducible on any number of threads.\nMetrics: QD score, coverage, continuous QD score, and hypervolume for several objectives.\nPlots through a Makie extension, tables of elites through a Tables.jl extension, GPU archives through a KernelAbstractions extension, and gradients through a ForwardDiff extension.\nConformance checks in QualityDiversity.Testing for the types you define.","title":"What is included"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"The MAP-Elites tutorial walks through a first run.\nA custom genotype evolves graphs.\nColour pairs and emotions, an extra example, evolves pairs of colours from calm to arousing and from sad to happy.\nConcepts explains the parts of the package and how a run proceeds.\nChoosing an algorithm compares the algorithms and archives.\nComing from pyribs or QDax maps their names to this package.\nVerification lists how each component is checked against pyribs, QDax and the papers.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"api/algorithms/#Algorithms-and-solving","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"An algorithm is an archive and a tuple of emitters. The constructors below assemble the usual combinations; QDAlgorithm takes any other.","title":"Algorithms and solving"},{"category":"section","location":"api/algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/algorithms/#Running","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"","title":"Running"},{"category":"type","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.QDAlgorithm","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"QDAlgorithm(archive, emitters)\n\nA quality-diversity algorithm: an archive and a tuple of emitters. Every algorithm shares one ask → evaluate → add → tell loop; they differ in which archive and emitters they combine.\n\nemitters is a Tuple so that the loop over emitters is unrolled and type-stable. An emitter with state may appear in it once: it keeps the batch it asked for until tell!, so one instance cannot run twice in a round. Build several alike with a comprehension.\n\nThe algorithm runs through the CommonSolve.jl interface: init creates a state, step! performs one round, solve! runs to the budget, and solve does init and solve! in one call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.QDAlgorithm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.MAPElites","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"MAPElites(prob; dims, bounds=nothing, batch_size=64, variation=GaussianMutation(0.1),\n          genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, outside=ClampOutside(),\n          phenotype=Nothing, device=nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nMAP-Elites: an archive laid out by the problem's descriptor space, and one VariationEmitter that applies the variation operator to batch_size children per round, with parents chosen uniformly from the archive. variation can be a mutation such as GaussianMutation or a crossover such as IsoLineVariation.\n\nFor a problem with descriptor_bounds, the archive is a GridArchive with dims cells along each descriptor dimension, laid out over bounds, by default the bounds of the problem; dims is required. For a problem with descriptor_categories, it is a CategoricalArchive with one cell for each combination of categories; dims and bounds do not apply and are an error. outside and phenotype are passed to the archive; see GridArchive and CategoricalArchive. A descriptor outside the bounds goes to the nearest cell by default, as in pyribs and QDax, so that a random initial population never stops a run; pass outside=ThrowOutside() to catch a descriptor function that does not match its bounds. A category has no nearest cell, so the default for a problem with categories is ThrowOutside(). A problem that returns a phenotype needs phenotype set to its type. The archive stores genotypes of the type that random_solution returns, see default_genotype; genotype overrides it.\n\nEverything runs on the host unless device names an array type, such as CuArray: then the archive is a DeviceGridArchive in arrays of that type and the emitter a DeviceVariationEmitter, so the variation, the evaluation and the insertion of a batch stay on the device. That needs a problem with descriptor_bounds, real-vector genotypes, no phenotype, and a variation that the device emitter supports.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\nresult = solve(prob, MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20)); budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\ncoverage(result.archive)\n\n\n\n\n\nMAPElites(archive::AbstractArchive; batch_size=64, variation=GaussianMutation(0.1))\n    -> QDAlgorithm\n\nMAP-Elites over any archive: archive with one VariationEmitter that applies variation to batch_size children per round, or a DeviceVariationEmitter for a DeviceCellArchive. Use it to run MAP-Elites with an archive other than the grid, such as a CVTArchive or a DeviceCVTArchive.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\narchive = CVTArchive(prob; cells=500, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nresult = solve(prob, MAPElites(archive); budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(2))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.MAPElites"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.CMAME","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"CMAME(prob; dims, sigma0, num_emitters=5, batch_size=<CMA-ES default>,\n      ranker=TwoStageImprovementRanker(), selection=FilterSelection(),\n      restart=NoImprovementRestart(), x0=nothing, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf,\n      bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64,\n      outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing, device=nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nCMA-ME (Fontaine et al., 2020): a GridArchive with dims cells along each descriptor dimension, as for MAPElites, and num_emitters independent CMAEmitters, each with its own search distribution. The emitters are the improvement emitter of the paper: candidates ranked by what the archive did with them, the ones the archive accepted recombined, and a restart from a random elite when a batch is rejected entirely. The paper runs 15 emitters of 36 candidates; the default is 5 emitters with the batch size of CMA-ES, 4 + floor(3 log(dim)), so pass num_emitters=15, batch_size=36 for its settings.\n\nsigma0 is the initial step size of the search distributions and has no default, because it depends on the scale of the solutions. The solutions must be real vectors; their length is that of random_solution(rng, prob). lower and upper bound the solutions, and x0 gives the mean of the first distribution of every emitter, by default a random elite. The other arguments are those of MAPElites and CMAEmitter.\n\nWith device, an array type such as CuArray, the archive is a DeviceGridArchive and the emitters are the searches of one DeviceCMAEmitter: all searches ask together, and their candidates are evaluated and added on the device as one batch, so each search sees the archive as it was before the round. That pays off with many emitters, hundreds rather than five.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\nresult = solve(prob, CMAME(prob; dims=(20, 20), sigma0=1.0); budget=50_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nqd_score(result.archive; offset=fitness_offset(prob))\n\n\n\n\n\nCMAME(archive::AbstractArchive; sigma0, dim, num_emitters=5, kwargs...) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nCMA-ME over any archive, such as a CVTArchive: archive with num_emitters CMAEmitters for solutions of length dim. The keyword arguments are those of CMAEmitter. For a DeviceCellArchive the emitters are the num_emitters searches of one DeviceCMAEmitter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAME"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.CMAMAE","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"CMAMAE(prob; dims, sigma0, threshold_min, learning_rate=0.01, num_emitters=5,\n       batch_size=<CMA-ES default>, ranker=ImprovementRanker(),\n       selection=MuSelection(), restart=ConvergenceRestart(), x0=nothing,\n       lower=-Inf, upper=Inf, bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n       fitness=Float64, outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nCMA-MAE (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2023): CMAEmitters as for CMAME but ranking candidates by their improvement over the threshold of their cell, recombining the best half, and restarting only on convergence, over a ThresholdArchive around a GridArchive. threshold_min, the lowest fitness the problem can produce, and learning_rate are those of the ThresholdArchive. Because the elites of that archive can get worse, solve returns its result archive, which holds the best solution of every cell. The paper runs 15 emitters of 36 candidates; see CMAME for the default. The other arguments are those of CMAME, without device: the threshold archive runs on the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAMAE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.CMAMEGA","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"CMAMEGA(prob; dims, sigma0, optimizer=AdamAscent(), num_emitters=1,\n        batch_size=<CMA-ES default>, ranker=TwoStageImprovementRanker(),\n        selection=FilterSelection(), restart=NoImprovementRestart(), x0=nothing,\n        normalize=true, bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64,\n        outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nCMA-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021, \"Differentiable Quality Diversity\"): a GridArchive with dims cells along each descriptor dimension, as for MAPElites, and num_emitters GradientArborescenceEmitters, which step along the gradients of the fitness and the descriptor. The problem must provide them; see evaluate_gradient. The ranking, selection and restart rules and the optimizer are those of CMA-MEGA (Adam) in the paper, which runs one emitter of 36 candidates. The default batch size is that of CMA-ES over the N + 1 gradient coefficients, 4 + floor(3 log(N + 1)) for N descriptor dimensions, as in pyribs; pass batch_size=36 for the setting of the paper.\n\nsigma0 is the initial step size of the distribution of the gradient coefficients and has no default; the paper uses 10 on the sphere and Rastrigin benchmarks. The other arguments are those of MAPElites and GradientArborescenceEmitter.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Sphere(100)    # gradients in closed form\nresult = solve(prob, CMAMEGA(prob; dims=(100, 100), sigma0=10.0); budget=20_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\ncoverage(result.archive)\n\n\n\n\n\nCMAMEGA(archive::AbstractArchive; sigma0, dim, num_emitters=1, kwargs...) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nCMA-MEGA over any archive with SVector descriptors, such as a CVTArchive: archive with num_emitters GradientArborescenceEmitters for solutions of length dim. The keyword arguments are those of GradientArborescenceEmitter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAMEGA"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.CMAMAEGA","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"CMAMAEGA(prob; dims, sigma0, threshold_min, learning_rate=0.01, optimizer=AdamAscent(),\n         num_emitters=1, batch_size=<CMA-ES default>, ranker=ImprovementRanker(),\n         selection=MuSelection(), restart=ConvergenceRestart(), x0=nothing,\n         normalize=true, bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64,\n         outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nCMA-MAEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2023): CMAMEGA with the rules of CMAMAE: candidates ranked by their improvement over the threshold of their cell, the best half recombined, restarts on convergence only, over a ThresholdArchive around a GridArchive. threshold_min and learning_rate are those of the ThresholdArchive; optimizer, the rule that moves the solution point, is that of the GradientArborescenceEmitters. solve returns the result archive, which holds the best solution of every cell. The default batch size is that of CMAMEGA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.CMAMAEGA"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.OMGMEGA","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"OMGMEGA(prob; dims, sigma0, batch_size=64, normalize=true, bounds=nothing,\n        genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, outside=ClampOutside(),\n        phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nOMG-MEGA (Fontaine and Nikolaidis, 2021, \"Differentiable Quality Diversity\"): MAP-Elites whose children are gradient steps from their parents, a GradientArchive around a GridArchive with dims cells along each descriptor dimension and a GradientOperatorEmitter. Every solution of the run is evaluated with its gradients; see evaluate_gradient. sigma0, the standard deviation of the gradient coefficients, has no default; the paper uses 10 on the sphere and Rastrigin benchmarks. The paper draws 36 children per round; the default batch_size=64 is that of MAPElites. The other arguments are those of MAPElites and GradientOperatorEmitter.\n\n\n\n\n\nOMGMEGA(archive::GradientArchive; sigma0, batch_size=64, normalize=true) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nOMG-MEGA over any GradientArchive, such as one around a CVTArchive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.OMGMEGA"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.DDS","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"DDS(prob; dims, sigma0, bandwidth, buffer_capacity=10_000, rng=Random.default_rng(),\n    num_emitters=15, batch_size=36, ranker=ImprovementRanker(), selection=MuSelection(),\n    restart=ConvergenceRestart(), x0=nothing, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf, bounds=nothing,\n    genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64, outside=ClampOutside(),\n    phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nDensity Descent Search with a kernel density estimate (DDS-KDE; Lee et al., 2024): a DensityArchive around a GridArchive with dims cells, and num_emitters CMAEmitters that rank their candidates by the density of the descriptors found so far, lowest first, recombine the best half and restart on convergence, the settings of the paper. It searches for diversity alone: the fitness only decides which solution the grid keeps in a cell. bandwidth, buffer_capacity and rng, which drives the buffer, are the arguments of the DensityArchive. For a reproducible run, seed DDS as well as solve, e.g. rng=Xoshiro(1): without it, rng is Random.default_rng(), which the seed of solve does not set. The other arguments are those of CMAME, without device: the density archive runs on the host.\n\nDensity descent drives the searches toward empty regions, also out of the region where the problem's descriptors change. On a problem that clips its solutions to a box, such as Rastrigin, a search outside the box sees the descriptors of the edge of the box, and with the default strategy it seldom converges, so it seldom restarts. Pass the box as lower and upper to keep the searches inside. With strategy=QualityDiversity.PyribsCMAES such a search restarts, as in pyribs: its rank-one covariance term grows with the square of the step size, and the covariance matrix becomes ill-conditioned.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.Rastrigin(10)\nalg = DDS(prob; dims=(20, 20), sigma0=0.5, bandwidth=0.5, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=50_000, rng=Xoshiro(2))\ncoverage(result.archive)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.DDS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.MOME","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"MOME(prob; dims, reference_point=reference_point(prob), max_front=50, batch_size=64,\n     variation=GaussianMutation(0.1), bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob),\n     outside=ClampOutside(), duplicates=RejectDuplicates(), full_front=RejectNewcomer(),\n     phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nMulti-objective MAP-Elites (Pierrot et al., 2022): a ParetoGridArchive, which keeps a Pareto front of at most max_front solutions in each cell of a grid with dims cells along each descriptor dimension, and one VariationEmitter that applies variation to batch_size children per round. A parent is a solution drawn uniformly from the front of a cell drawn uniformly among the occupied ones. The problem returns a vector of objectives as its fitness. reference_point is the corner from which hypervolume is measured; a problem can supply it with reference_point. duplicates and full_front decide about a solution whose objectives equal those of a stored one and about one for a full front; see ParetoGridArchive. With full_front=QualityDiversity.ReplaceCrowded() the archive keeps fronts as MOME-PGX does (Janmohamed et al., 2023), whose crowding-based selection of parents and policy-gradient variation are not part of this constructor. The default variation is that of MAPElites; the MOME paper uses polynomial mutation with eta=1 together with a crossover on its Rastrigin benchmarks, and variation=PolynomialMutation(lower, upper; eta=1) is the mutation alone.\n\nsolve returns the archive, and a run records its MOQD score, coverage, the largest hypervolume of a cell and the number of solutions.\n\nExample\n\nprob = QualityDiversity.Benchmarks.BiSphere(10)\nresult = solve(prob, MOME(prob; dims=(20, 20)); budget=50_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nqd_score(result.archive), global_hypervolume(result.archive)\n\n\n\n\n\nMOME(archive::ParetoGridArchive; batch_size=64, variation=GaussianMutation(0.1)) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nMOME over a given archive: archive with one VariationEmitter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.MOME"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.MELS","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"MELS(prob::SampledSpread; dims, batch_size=64, variation=GaussianMutation(0.1),\n     bounds=nothing, genotype=default_genotype(prob), fitness=Float64,\n     outside=ClampOutside(), phenotype=Nothing) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nMAP-Elites with low spread (Mace et al., 2023): MAP-Elites for a noisy problem in which an elite is replaced only by a solution with higher fitness and no larger spread, the mean distance between the descriptors of its evaluations. The archive is a SpreadArchive around a GridArchive with dims cells along each descriptor dimension, and one VariationEmitter applies variation to batch_size children per round.\n\nprob is a SampledSpread problem, which evaluates every solution num_samples times; pass the same problem to solve. The other arguments are those of MAPElites.\n\nExample\n\nnoisy = StochasticQDProblem((rng, x) -> (-sum(abs2, x), (x[1], x[2]) .+ 0.05 .* randn(rng, 2));\n                            init=rng -> randn(rng, 5), bounds=((-3, 3), (-3, 3)))\nprob = SampledSpread(noisy; num_samples=10)\nresult = solve(prob, MELS(prob; dims=(20, 20)); budget=200_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\n\n\n\n\n\nMELS(archive::SpreadArchive; batch_size=64, variation=GaussianMutation(0.1)) -> QDAlgorithm\n\nMELS over a given archive, such as a SpreadArchive around a CVTArchive: archive with one VariationEmitter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.MELS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#CommonSolve.solve","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"solve(prob, alg::QDAlgorithm; budget, rng=Random.default_rng(), callback=nothing,\n      init_size) -> QDResult\n\nRun alg on prob until budget evaluations are spent: solve!(init(prob, alg; ...)). The keyword arguments are those of init. alg is not modified, so it can be solved again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#CommonSolve.init","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"init(prob, alg::QDAlgorithm; budget, rng=Random.default_rng(), callback=nothing,\n     init_size=<sum of the emitters' batch sizes>) -> QDState\n\nPrepare a run. The archive and emitters of alg are copied, so alg is not modified, and the copy is filled with init_size solutions from random_solution.\n\nbudget is the number of evaluations, including the initial ones. A problem that evaluates a solution several times, such as Sampled, counts every one. A run stops at the first step that reaches it, so it can overshoot by less than one round of emitter batches. callback(state) is called after initialization and after every step. Pass a seeded rng for a reproducible run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.init"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#CommonSolve.step!","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"step!(state::QDState)\n\nOne round: for each emitter in turn, ask for candidates, evaluate them, add them to the archive, and tell the emitter the outcome. Then record the metrics and call the callback. An emitter that needs gradients first gets the points it asks for with ask_gradients evaluated with their gradients and added to the archive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/algorithms/#CommonSolve.solve!","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"solve!(state::QDState) -> QDResult\n\nStep until the evaluation budget is reached. Returns immediately if it already has been.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.QDState","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"QDState\n\nState of a running algorithm, created by init(prob, alg; ...). It holds the problem, working copies of the algorithm's archive and emitters, the random number generator, the number of evaluations so far, and history, a vector with the metrics of the result archive after initialization and after every step.\n\nFields are read with state.archive, state.n_evals, state.history, and so on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.QDState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/algorithms/#QualityDiversity.QDResult","page":"Algorithms and solving","text":"QDResult\n\nOutcome of solve: the archive of the best solutions found, which is result_archive of the algorithm's archive, the metrics history, and the number of evaluations n_evals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.QDResult"},{"category":"section","location":"api/testing/#Testing","page":"Testing","text":"QualityDiversity.Testing checks that a new problem, archive, variation or emitter meets its contract. It depends only on the Test standard library, so a package that defines such a type can call it from its own tests; see Extending the package.","title":"Testing"},{"category":"module","location":"api/testing/#QualityDiversity.Testing","page":"Testing","text":"QualityDiversity.Testing\n\nConformance checks for the types a user defines. Each function runs inside a @testset, so failures are reported by the test runner of the caller:\n\nusing Test, QualityDiversity\n@testset \"MyProblem\" begin\n    QualityDiversity.Testing.test_problem(MyProblem())\nend\n\nThe checks cover the contract of each role, whether the calls are inferrable, that the results have the right types, and, for archives, that cell_index (and add! for isbits genotypes) does not allocate.\n\ntest_problem\ntest_archive\ntest_front_archive\ntest_variation\ntest_emitter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Testing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/testing/#QualityDiversity.Testing.test_problem","page":"Testing","text":"test_problem(prob; rng=Xoshiro(1), n=5, rtol=0)\n\nCheck that prob meets the contract of AbstractQDProblem, or of AbstractStochasticQDProblem.\n\nrandom_solution is called n times. Every solution must evaluate to an Evaluation with a real or vector fitness and a descriptor that lies inside descriptor_bounds(prob) and has one entry per bound. For a problem that defines descriptor_categories(prob) instead, the descriptor must be a tuple with one entry equal to a category of each dimension. evaluate must be inferrable, deterministic, and agree with evaluate_batch.\n\nevaluate_batch must give exactly the results of evaluate unless rtol is positive. A problem that evaluates batches on a GPU, in lower precision, agrees only up to rounding: with rtol the fitness and descriptor values then have to match within the relative tolerance rtol, and within rtol in absolute terms for values near zero.\n\nFor a stochastic problem, evaluate(rng, prob, x) must give the same result for the same seed, evaluate(prob, x) without a generator must throw, and evaluate_batch(rng, prob, xs) must be reproducible from the generator. rtol does not apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Testing.test_problem"},{"category":"function","location":"api/testing/#QualityDiversity.Testing.test_archive","page":"Testing","text":"test_archive(make_archive, prob; rng=Xoshiro(1), n=200, check_allocations=true, elitist=true,\n             sequential_batch=true, population=false, gain=true)\n\nCheck that the archives built by make_archive() meet the contract of AbstractArchive. make_archive is called without arguments and must return a new, empty archive each time. prob supplies solutions and evaluations; its descriptors must lie inside the archive's bounds. A stochastic problem is evaluated with a fixed seed.\n\nThe checks cover an empty archive, the status and improvement of each kind of insertion, length, capacity, cell_index (on an archive holding all n solutions), sample_elites, elites, add_batch!, and empty! when the archive defines it. elites must return the phenotype that came with each stored genotype, of the archive's phenotype type. A fitness that is NaN or infinite must throw an ArgumentError from add! and from add_batch! before the archive changes. With check_allocations, cell_index must not allocate, and neither must add! when the genotype type is isbits.\n\nAn archive is elitist if a cell only accepts a solution with higher fitness than its occupant, so that adding the same solution again, or a worse one, is Rejected. An archive whose cells accept solutions below the occupant, such as a ThresholdArchive with a finite threshold_min and a learning_rate below 1, passes elitist=false. The checks then require only that a status agrees with the sign of improvement: Rejected for zero or less, Improved for more than zero.\n\nBy default add_batch! must give the results of add! on each solution in order. An archive that computes the results of a batch against its state before the batch, such as a GridArchive or a ThresholdArchive, passes sequential_batch=false. The checks then require that the results are valid and that the result of each solution equals that of adding the solution alone to the archive as it was before the batch.\n\nAn archive that chooses its whole population again from the stored solutions and the batch, such as a DNSArchive, passes population=true. A solution then has no cell to improve, so the checks require only that each result is NewCell or Rejected, that the solutions of a batch added to an empty archive that are NewCell are the ones it stores, and that the archive never holds more than its capacity.\n\nBy default improvement is a gain in fitness: positive for an Improved solution and zero or less for a Rejected one. An archive that reports another quantity there, such as a DensityArchive, whose improvement is the negated density, passes gain=false, and the checks ignore the value of improvement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Testing.test_archive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/testing/#QualityDiversity.Testing.test_front_archive","page":"Testing","text":"test_front_archive(make_archive, prob; rng=Xoshiro(1), n=200, check_allocations=true)\n\nCheck that the archives built by make_archive() meet the contract of an archive that keeps a Pareto front in every cell, such as a ParetoGridArchive. prob is a problem with a vector fitness whose solutions are evaluated for the checks, and make_archive() returns an empty archive for it whose reference point lies below the fitness values of prob. The fitness type of the archive must be a vector of reals.\n\nThe result of add! must be NewCell for an empty cell, Rejected with zero improvement for a solution that a stored one matches in every objective, and Improved with a positive improvement for a solution that beats the front. Every cell must hold a front of solutions none of which beats another, length counts the occupied cells and num_solutions the solutions that elites yields. add_batch! must give each solution the result that it has alone against the archive as it was before the batch, and leave the archive as adding the solutions one at a time does. An objective that is NaN or infinite must throw an ArgumentError from add! and from add_batch! before the archive changes. sample_elites must draw stored genotypes, inferrably, and reproducibly from the generator. elites must return the phenotype that came with each stored genotype. The metrics must agree with the hypervolume of the fronts. add! must not allocate for a rejected solution with an isbits genotype and two objectives when check_allocations is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Testing.test_front_archive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/testing/#QualityDiversity.Testing.test_variation","page":"Testing","text":"test_variation(op, parent; parent2=nothing, rng=Xoshiro(1))\n\nCheck that the variation operator op meets the contract of AbstractVariation for the genotype parent. Without parent2 the operator is a mutation, vary(rng, op, parent), and n_parents(op) must be 1; with it, a crossover, vary(rng, op, parent, parent2), and n_parents(op) must be 2.\n\nThe call must be inferrable, leave its parents unchanged, and give the same child for the same RNG state. A new child must not be the parent object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Testing.test_variation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/testing/#QualityDiversity.Testing.test_emitter","page":"Testing","text":"test_emitter(emitter, archive, prob; rng=Xoshiro(1))\n\nCheck that emitter meets the contract of AbstractEmitter. archive must be an archive for prob that already holds at least one solution. The check modifies both emitter and archive.\n\nbatch_size must be a positive integer, and ask must return that many genotypes of the archive's genotype type, inferrably, as a Vector, or as a DeviceBatch for a DeviceCellArchive, none of them an object the archive stores. The batch must then pass through evaluate_batch, add_batch! and tell!.\n\nAn emitter whose ask_gradients returns solutions first gets them back through evaluate_gradient_batch, add_batch! and tell_gradients!, as in step!. For a GradientArchive every batch is evaluated with its gradients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QualityDiversity.Testing.test_emitter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/heatmap/#Plotting-archives","page":"Plotting archives","text":"Plotting lives in a package extension: loading a Makie backend, such as CairoMakie or GLMakie, adds plot recipes for archives, and the package gains no plotting functions of its own. The recipes are\n\nheatmap(archive) for an archive with two descriptor dimensions, and scatter(archive) for an UnstructuredArchive,\necdfplot(archive) for the distribution of the elite fitness, and\nlines(cqd_score(...)) for the continuous QD score against its penalty weight.\n\nRun it from the repository root, after the one-time setup\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nwith\n\njulia --project=examples examples/heatmap.jl\n\nIt saves the figures next to this file.\n\nusing QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nusing CairoMakie\nusing Random: Xoshiro","title":"Plotting archives"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/heatmap/#Two-archives","page":"Plotting archives","text":"The same problem and budget, once with a grid and once with a centroidal Voronoi tessellation (CVT) of 1000 cells.\n\nprob = Rastrigin(10)\nbounds = QualityDiversity.descriptor_bounds(prob)\nvariation = GaussianMutation(0.5)\ngrid = solve(prob, MAPElites(prob; dims=(40, 40), batch_size=100, variation); budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\ncvt = solve(prob, MAPElites(CVTArchive(1_000, bounds; rng=Xoshiro(1)); batch_size=100, variation);\n            budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nQDResult after 100000 evaluations\n  archive: CVTArchive with 1000 of 1000 cells occupied\n  qd_score: 265114.70934312104\n  coverage: 1.0\n  max_fitness: -34.367035149932164\n  mean_fitness: -144.88529065687894","title":"Two archives"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/heatmap/#Heatmaps","page":"Plotting archives","text":"heatmap!(ax, archive) draws the fitness of every cell; empty cells are transparent. A grid shows one rectangle per cell, a CVT archive its Voronoi cells, and a CategoricalArchive one square per pair of categories, with the category values as the axis labels. Archives that wrap another, such as a ThresholdArchive, show the archive of their best solutions.\n\nfig = Figure(size=(1000, 420))\nfor (column, (title, result)) in enumerate((\"GridArchive\" => grid, \"CVTArchive\" => cvt))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 2*column-1]; title, xlabel=\"descriptor 1\", ylabel=\"descriptor 2\", aspect=1)\n    hm = heatmap!(ax, result.archive)\n    Colorbar(fig[1, 2*column], hm; label=\"fitness\")\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Heatmaps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/heatmap/#Distribution-of-the-elite-fitness","page":"Plotting archives","text":"ecdfplot shows, for each fitness value, the fraction of elites at or below it. Unlike the QD score it does not sum over the cells, so it compares archives of different sizes.\n\nfig = Figure(size=(500, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"fitness\", ylabel=\"fraction of elites\")\necdfplot!(ax, grid.archive; label=\"GridArchive\")\necdfplot!(ax, cvt.archive; label=\"CVTArchive\")\naxislegend(ax; position=:lt)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Distribution of the elite fitness"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/heatmap/#Metrics-over-a-run","page":"Plotting archives","text":"result.history holds the metrics after every round, so the progress of a run is a line plot.\n\nfig = Figure(size=(500, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"evaluations\", ylabel=\"QD score\")\nfor (label, result) in (\"GridArchive\" => grid, \"CVTArchive\" => cvt)\n    lines!(ax, [r.n_evals for r in result.history], [r.qd_score for r in result.history]; label)\nend\naxislegend(ax; position=:rb)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Metrics over a run"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/heatmap/#Continuous-QD-score","page":"Plotting archives","text":"The QD score depends on the cells of an archive. The continuous QD score (Kent et al., 2022) does not: it asks, for random target descriptors, how good the elite that the archive recommends is, with a penalty for its distance from the target that grows with a weight θ. lines(result) plots the mean score against θ.\n\nfitness_range = (-QualityDiversity.fitness_offset(prob), 0.0)    # the range of Rastrigin(10)\nfig = Figure(size=(500, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"penalty weight θ\", ylabel=\"mean ω over the targets\")\nfor (label, result) in (\"GridArchive\" => grid, \"CVTArchive\" => cvt)\n    score = cqd_score(Xoshiro(2), result.archive; num_targets=200, fitness_range)\n    lines!(ax, score; label)\nend\naxislegend(ax)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Continuous QD score"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/comparison/#Coming-from-pyribs-or-QDax","page":"Coming from pyribs or QDax","text":"pyribs and QDax are the two main quality-diversity libraries in Python. This package offers most of their algorithms, under the names below, and checks several of them against those libraries on the same inputs (test/reference; see Verification).","title":"Coming from pyribs or QDax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/comparison/#Names","page":"Coming from pyribs or QDax","text":"QualityDiversity.jl pyribs QDax\nGridArchive GridArchive MapElitesRepertoire with grid centroids\nCVTArchive CVTArchive MapElitesRepertoire with CVT centroids\nSlidingBoundariesArchive SlidingBoundariesArchive —\nCategoricalArchive CategoricalArchive —\nUnstructuredArchive ProximityArchive UnstructuredRepertoire\nDNSArchive DNSArchive DominatedNoveltyRepertoire\nThresholdArchive GridArchive(learning_rate=...) —\nDensityArchive DensityArchive —\nSpreadArchive — MELSRepertoire\nParetoGridArchive — MOMERepertoire\nVariationEmitter GaussianEmitter, IsoLineEmitter MixingEmitter\nCMAEmitter EvolutionStrategyEmitter CMAEmitter and its subclasses\nBanditScheduler BanditScheduler —\nCMAES, SepCMAES, LMMAES, OpenAIES CMAEvolutionStrategy, SeparableCMAEvolutionStrategy, LMMAEvolutionStrategy, OpenAIEvolutionStrategy CMAES\nImprovementRanker, TwoStageImprovementRanker, … ImprovementRanker, TwoStageImprovementRanker, … emitter subclasses\nsolve a loop over scheduler.ask() and scheduler.tell() a loop over map_elites.update, or scan_update\ncqd_score cqd_score —\nheatmap(archive) (Makie) grid_archive_heatmap, cvt_archive_heatmap plot_2d_map_elites_repertoire\necdfplot(archive) (Makie) archive_ecdf —\n\nWhere the libraries differ in a rule, such as how a batch is inserted or how ties are broken, the docstring of the type says which rule this package follows and why.","title":"Names"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/comparison/#Differences-in-approach","page":"Coming from pyribs or QDax","text":"Genotypes. pyribs and QDax store solutions as arrays of numbers. Here a solution can be any Julia type, such as a graph or a molecule, with a vary method for it; see the graph tutorial.\nFitness is maximized, as in both libraries.\nOne loop. An algorithm is an archive and a tuple of emitters, run by solve or one round at a time with step!.\nRandomness. Every function that draws random numbers takes the generator, and a run is reproducible from its seed, also with several threads.","title":"Differences in approach"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/comparison/#Speed","page":"Coming from pyribs or QDax","text":"benchmark/compare runs the same problems in all three libraries: MAP-Elites, CMA-ME and CMA-MAE on Rastrigin with 20 parameters and a 100 × 100 grid, MOME, and MAP-Elites and CMA-ME on a GPU. Its README gives the settings, the data of every run and how to reproduce them. In short, on one CPU core:\n\nMAP-Elites is 2 to 14 times faster than pyribs, most at small batches. It is faster than QDax at batches of 100 and below; at 1000 and above QDax, compiled with JAX and with a grid lookup in place of its centroid search, is 1.2 to 1.35 times slower in float64 and 1.3 to 1.7 times faster in float32;\nCMA-ME is 5 to 7 times faster than pyribs and CMA-MAE 6 times;\non a GPU at a batch of 10 000, MAP-Elites with a grid is level with QDax in float32 and 1.35 times slower in float64; with a CVT archive QDax is 2.7 times faster in float32 and 2.5 times slower in float64.\n\n(Image: MAP-Elites: time against batch size on one CPU core and on a GPU, in float64 and float32)\n\n(Image: Median time of MAP-Elites, CMA-ME, CMA-MAE and MOME on one CPU core and of MAP-Elites on a GPU, for QualityDiversity.jl, pyribs and QDax)\n\nThe objective in these benchmarks takes nanoseconds, so the numbers measure the library. With an expensive objective the evaluation dominates and the libraries differ little.","title":"Speed"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/mome/#Several-objectives","page":"Several objectives","text":"With several objectives there is rarely one best solution, only trade-offs: a solution is on the Pareto front if no other solution is at least as good in every objective and better in one. Multi-objective MAP-Elites (MOME; Pierrot et al.,\n\nkeeps such a front in every cell of a grid, so the archive answers \"what\n\nare the best trade-offs for each kind of solution?\".\n\nSet up once, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nRun:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/mome.jl\n\nusing QualityDiversity, QualityDiversity.Benchmarks\nusing Random: Xoshiro","title":"Several objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/mome/#The-problem","page":"Several objectives","text":"BiSphere(10) has two objectives over 10 parameters, the negated squared distances to the points (1, …, 1) and (-1, …, -1). No solution is best in both. Its descriptor is that of Rastrigin: the mean of the first five and of the last five parameters.\n\nThe fitness of a multi-objective problem is a vector, here an SVector of two numbers, and all objectives are maximized.\n\nprob = BiSphere(10)\nev = QualityDiversity.evaluate(prob, zeros(10))\nev.fitness\n2-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n -10.0\n -10.0\n\nHypervolume, the size of the region of objective space that a front dominates, is measured from a reference point below the values of interest. A problem can supply it, as BiSphere does:\n\nQualityDiversity.reference_point(prob)\n2-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n -374.544\n -374.544","title":"The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/mome/#Running-MOME","page":"Several objectives","text":"MOME builds a ParetoGridArchive, which keeps a front of at most max_front solutions in each cell, and a variation emitter that draws a parent uniformly from the front of a random occupied cell.\n\nalg = MOME(prob; dims=(20, 20), max_front=50, batch_size=64, variation=GaussianMutation(0.2))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\narchive = result.archive","title":"Running MOME"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/mome/#Reading-the-results","page":"Several objectives","text":"length counts the occupied cells and QualityDiversity.num_solutions the solutions in all fronts. The metrics of a multi-objective run differ from those of MAP-Elites: qd_score is the MOQD score, the sum of the hypervolumes of the fronts of all cells, and global_hypervolume is that of the front of all solutions together.\n\nfinal = result.history[end]\n(occupied_cells=length(archive), solutions=final.num_solutions, moqd_score=final.qd_score,\n global_hypervolume=global_hypervolume(archive))\n(occupied_cells = 146, solutions = 1491, moqd_score = 1.6680794262396818e7, global_hypervolume = 139931.32893777487)\n\nQualityDiversity.elites iterates over every solution of every front, with its evaluation. QualityDiversity.max_scores gives the best value found for each objective:\n\nQualityDiversity.max_scores(archive)\n2-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n -0.07303377350834903\n -0.12021962838541067","title":"Reading the results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/mome/#Plotting","page":"Several objectives","text":"With a Makie backend loaded, heatmap(archive) shows the hypervolume of the front in each cell. The second panel shows every solution of the archive in objective space: the fronts of the cells near the center of descriptor space reach the line between the two optima.\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(size=(900, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title=\"hypervolume per cell\", xlabel=\"descriptor 1\", ylabel=\"descriptor 2\", aspect=1)\nhm = heatmap!(ax, archive)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm)\nobjectives = [ev.fitness for (_, ev) in QualityDiversity.elites(archive)]\nscatter(fig[1, 3], first.(objectives), last.(objectives); markersize=3,\n        axis=(title=\"all solutions\", xlabel=\"objective 1\", ylabel=\"objective 2\"))\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Verification","page":"Verification","text":"This page lists, for every component, how it is checked against code written by others: pyribs 0.12.0, QDax 0.5.0, pymoo, MOME-PGX, or the equations of the paper. Where the result differs, the table says why. Items that need more work link to an issue.","title":"Verification"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Methods","page":"Verification","text":"The table names the strongest check that passes.\n\nMethod What is compared\nReference The same scripted inputs go through the other library and through this package, and the outputs agree exactly or to rounding: statuses, improvements, kept solutions, cells, metrics, children. A Python script in test/reference writes the other library's outputs, and a test compares them on every run of the test suite.\nPaper The update equations of the paper, written independently in NumPy (test/reference/generate_strategies.py), run on fixed inputs. Used where a library departs from the paper.\nDistribution For an operator that draws random numbers, which no two libraries draw alike: the mean, standard deviation, quantiles and correlation of 200 000 children of fixed parents.\nWhole run A full run in two libraries with the same settings, in benchmark/compare; coverage and QD score agree within the spread between seeds. This catches gross errors, not small ones.\nInternal Two paths of this package agree: device and host, ForwardDiff and closed-form gradients.","title":"Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Archives","page":"Verification","text":"Component Compared with Method Result\nGridArchive pyribs GridArchive, QDax MapElitesRepertoire Reference Statuses, improvements, cells and kept solutions after every batch equal pyribs; kept solutions equal QDax.\nCVTArchive pyribs CVTArchive, QDax get_cells_indices Reference The same cell for every descriptor (pyribs clips descriptors to the bounds first, QDax does not). The k-means centroids are not compared yet: #84.\nCategoricalArchive pyribs CategoricalArchive (QDax has none) Reference Equal.\nSlidingBoundariesArchive pyribs SlidingBoundariesArchive (QDax has none) Reference Equal up to fitness ties and pyribs's tolerance of 1e-6 below a boundary. pyribs 0.12.0 clips descriptors to the old bounds while it re-adds solutions during a remap; the reference corrects this.\nThresholdArchive pyribs GridArchive with learning_rate Reference Equal.\nUnstructuredArchive pyribs ProximityArchive (one neighbor, local competition); QDax UnstructuredRepertoire Reference (pyribs) Equal to pyribs, one solution at a time. QDax keeps far more solutions, closer together than its threshold: #81.\nDNSArchive pyribs DNSArchive, QDax DominatedNoveltyRepertoire Reference Equal to both.\nDensityArchive pyribs DensityArchive (QDax has none) Reference Equal densities. pyribs's reservoir sampling replaces the wrong elements when it replaces several in one batch; this package follows Li (1994). See DDS under Algorithms.\nParetoGridArchive QDax MOMERepertoire, MOME-PGX, pymoo Reference Equal fronts and hypervolumes. By default, solutions with equal fitness vectors are not stored twice (RejectDuplicates); KeepDuplicates gives QDax's behavior.\nSpreadArchive QDax MELSRepertoire Reference Equal.\nGradientArchive through OMG-MEGA, below Reference Equal.\nDeviceGridArchive, DeviceCVTArchive the host archives Internal The same cells as on the host, ties included.","title":"Archives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Variation","page":"Verification","text":"Component Compared with Method Result\nGaussianMutation pyribs GaussianOperator Distribution Same formula; distributions agree.\nIsoLineVariation pyribs IsoLineOperator, QDax isoline_variation Distribution Same formula; distributions agree with both.\nPolynomialMutation pymoo mut_pm (Deb's form); QDax polynomial_mutation Reference (pymoo) Equal to pymoo to 1e-13. QDax differs: it uses (x - lo)/span where Deb uses 1 - (x - lo)/span, draws its uniforms in Float32, and mutates exactly ⌊proportion·n⌋ entries instead of each with probability proportion.\nUniformCrossover QDax polynomial_crossover Distribution QDax draws ⌊proportion·n⌋ positions with replacement, so with proportion 0.5 it changes 41% of 10 entries; this package takes each entry from the second parent with probability proportion.","title":"Variation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Emitters-and-evolution-strategies","page":"Verification","text":"Component Compared with Method Result\nVariationEmitter pyribs, QDax Whole run MAP-Elites on Rastrigin agrees with both at every batch size.\nCMAEmitter: rankers, selection, restarts pyribs EvolutionStrategyEmitter Reference Statuses, ranking keys, parent counts and restarts equal for six rankers, both selection rules and three restart rules. Candidates with equal keys are ordered differently: this package puts the earlier one first, pyribs the later one (and, for the objective ranker, an arbitrary one).\nCMAES the CMA-ES tutorial (Hansen, 2016) Paper Equal. pyribs's CMA-ES departs from the tutorial in five places: the rank-one weight c1², an evolution path p_c not divided by σ, a step-size update (‖p_σ‖²/n − 1)/2, another h_σ test, and a lazily updated eigendecomposition.\nPyribsCMAES pyribs CMAEvolutionStrategy Reference Equal mean, step size, covariance and paths to 1e-15 over a short run and a 20-generation run, with the eigendecomposition recomputed in the same generations. It copies all five differences; see PyribsCMA.\nSepCMAES Ros and Hansen (2008) with pycma's constants Paper Equal. pyribs's sep-CMA-ES uses other learning rates than pycma, besides the differences of its CMA-ES: #83.\nLMMAES the paper; pyribs LMMAEvolutionStrategy Reference Equal to both.\nOpenAIES the paper; pyribs OpenAIEvolutionStrategy Reference Equal to both.\nBanditScheduler pyribs BanditScheduler Reference The same choice in all 230 rounds without a tie.\nGradientArborescenceEmitter pyribs GradientArborescenceEmitter Reference Equal branches, rankings, parent counts and points, with Adam and with gradient ascent.\nGradientOperatorEmitter pyribs GradientOperatorEmitter, QDax OMGMEGAEmitter Reference Equal children for the same parents and coefficients. pyribs also adds Gaussian noise to every parent before the gradient step (its sigma), which the paper and QDax do not; QDax's sigma_g is a variance.\nDeviceVariationEmitter, DeviceCMAEmitter QDax on a GPU; the host emitters Whole run, Internal Agree; the GPU runs are to be repeated with the current version: #85.","title":"Emitters and evolution strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Problems-and-metrics","page":"Verification","text":"Component Compared with Method Result\nRastrigin, BiSphere, Sphere the definitions in the comparison scripts Reference Equal.\nSampled QDax average, median Reference Equal.\nqd_score, coverage, max_fitness, mean_fitness pyribs archive statistics, QDax default_qd_metrics Reference Equal. pyribs subtracts its qd_score_offset from every fitness and QDax adds its qd_offset.\ncqd_score pyribs cqd_score Reference Equal.\nhypervolume pymoo Reference Equal to 1e-15.\nGradients from ForwardDiff closed forms of Sphere and Rastrigin Internal Equal.","title":"Problems and metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verification/#Algorithms","page":"Verification","text":"A whole run per algorithm against the library that has it, with the settings of both set equal.\n\nAlgorithm Compared with Result\nMAPElites pyribs, QDax Agree.\nCMAME pyribs, QDax Agree with pyribs for improvement, optimizing and random direction emitters. QDax recombines and restarts differently and reaches other coverage.\nCMAMAE pyribs Lower coverage than pyribs, from pyribs's covariance update; with strategy=PyribsCMAES it agrees.\nCMAMEGA pyribs Agree.\nMOME QDax Agree, with equal global hypervolume.\nMELS QDax QDax reaches slightly higher coverage and lower best fitness: #82.\nDDS pyribs With strategy=PyribsCMAES, agrees with pyribs once pyribs's reservoir sampling is corrected (coverage 0.89–0.90 against 0.87–0.92 on 20-dimensional Rastrigin); pyribs as released reaches 0.76–0.80. With the default CMAES, searches that leave the box of Rastrigin seldom restart (coverage 0.66–0.70); with the box as lower and upper, 0.90–0.92. pyribs also restarts every search after its first round, in which the buffer is empty and every batch has density zero.\n\nThe default settings of the algorithm constructors follow pyribs's emitter defaults, not always those of the papers; the docstring of each constructor gives the settings of its paper.","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy/#Noisy-problems","page":"Noisy problems","text":"Many problems do not give the same answer twice: a simulation with random initial conditions, a robot on an uneven floor, a measurement with noise. An archive that stores one evaluation per solution then fills with lucky solutions, whose single evaluation was better than their average, and with solutions in the wrong cell, whose descriptor happened to land there.\n\nThis tutorial shows the effect and the two remedies the package offers: averaging several evaluations with Sampled, and MAP-Elites with low spread (MELS), which also prefers solutions whose descriptor varies little.\n\nSet up once, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nRun:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/noisy.jl\n\nusing QualityDiversity\nusing Random: AbstractRNG, Xoshiro","title":"Noisy problems"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy/#A-noisy-problem","page":"Noisy problems","text":"The solutions are vectors of 6 numbers. The expected fitness is the negated squared norm, and every evaluation adds noise of standard deviation 1. The descriptor is the first two entries, clipped to the box, with noise of standard deviation 0.02 + |x[3]| / 2: the third entry decides how reliable the descriptor of a solution is, without changing its expected fitness.\n\nA stochastic problem is a function of a random number generator and a solution. It must draw every random number from that generator, which the run seeds, so that the run is reproducible from its seed.\n\nexpected_fitness(x) = -sum(abs2, x)\nexpected_descriptor(x) = [clamp(x[1], -2, 2), clamp(x[2], -2, 2)]\nmean(v) = sum(v) / length(v)\n\nfunction noisy_score(rng::AbstractRNG, x)\n    fitness = expected_fitness(x) + randn(rng)\n    spread = 0.02 + 0.5 * abs(x[3])\n    descriptor = expected_descriptor(x) .+ spread .* randn(rng, 2)\n    return fitness, (clamp(descriptor[1], -2, 2), clamp(descriptor[2], -2, 2))\nend\n\nnoisy = StochasticQDProblem(noisy_score; init=rng -> randn(rng, 6), bounds=((-2, 2), (-2, 2)))","title":"A noisy problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy/#How-good-are-the-elites-really?","page":"Noisy problems","text":"To judge an archive, every elite is evaluated without noise: its expected fitness is compared with the fitness the archive stored, and its expected descriptor with the cell it is stored in. judge returns the number of elites, their mean stored and mean expected fitness, and the fraction of elites in the wrong cell.\n\nfunction judge(archive)\n    elites = collect(QualityDiversity.elites(archive))\n    stored = [ev.fitness for (x, ev) in elites]\n    expected = [expected_fitness(x) for (x, ev) in elites]\n    misplaced = count(((x, ev),) -> QualityDiversity.cell_index(archive, expected_descriptor(x)) !=\n                                    QualityDiversity.cell_index(archive, ev.descriptor), elites)\n    return (elites=length(elites), stored_fitness=mean(stored), expected_fitness=mean(expected),\n            wrong_cell=misplaced / length(elites))\nend\njudge (generic function with 1 method)","title":"How good are the elites really?"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy/#One-evaluation-per-solution","page":"Noisy problems","text":"A stochastic problem works with every algorithm. Plain MAP-Elites evaluates each solution once.\n\nbudget = 200_000\ndims = (10, 10)\n\nplain = solve(noisy, MAPElites(noisy; dims); budget, rng=Xoshiro(1))\njudge(plain.archive)\n(elites = 100, stored_fitness = 1.5037823105235857, expected_fitness = -1.3060680789945724, wrong_cell = 0.93)\n\nThe stored fitness is far above the expected one: of many solutions with similar expected fitness, the archive keeps the one with the luckiest evaluation. And nearly every elite sits in a cell its expected descriptor does not belong to.","title":"One evaluation per solution"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy/#Averaging-several-evaluations","page":"Noisy problems","text":"Sampled evaluates every solution num_samples times and reports the mean fitness and the mean descriptor (expectation=QualityDiversity.Median() takes the median). Every evaluation counts against the budget, so the run scores ten times fewer solutions.\n\naveraged = Sampled(noisy; num_samples=10)\nmean_run = solve(averaged, MAPElites(averaged; dims); budget, rng=Xoshiro(1))\njudge(mean_run.archive)\n(elites = 100, stored_fitness = -1.9014388663785384, expected_fitness = -2.523235151098118, wrong_cell = 0.53)\n\nThe stored fitness is now much closer to the expected fitness, and fewer elites are in the wrong cell. The expected fitness is lower than with one evaluation, because the run tried ten times fewer solutions: averaging trades search for reliability.","title":"Averaging several evaluations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy/#Low-spread","page":"Noisy problems","text":"Averaging does not stop a solution with an unreliable descriptor from taking a cell. MAP-Elites with low spread (Mace et al., 2023) keeps every descriptor of the num_samples evaluations: the spread of a solution is the mean distance between them, and a solution replaces an elite only if it is fitter and its spread is no larger. SampledSpread evaluates the solutions, and MELS builds the algorithm around a SpreadArchive.\n\nspread_prob = SampledSpread(noisy; num_samples=10)\nmels = solve(spread_prob, MELS(spread_prob; dims); budget, rng=Xoshiro(1))\njudge(mels.archive)\n(elites = 100, stored_fitness = -2.7938589820775714, expected_fitness = -3.173796149440052, wrong_cell = 0.26)\n\nStill fewer elites of MELS are in the wrong cell, at some cost in fitness. Its elites have reliable descriptors: their third entry, which sets the descriptor noise, is smaller.\n\nmean_abs_x3(archive) = mean([abs(x[3]) for (x, _) in QualityDiversity.elites(archive)])\n(MELS=mean_abs_x3(mels.archive), mean_of_10=mean_abs_x3(mean_run.archive))\n(MELS = 0.15890557571251646, mean_of_10 = 0.359480134218161)","title":"Low spread"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/graphs/#A-custom-genotype:-graphs","page":"A custom genotype: graphs","text":"The genotype in the other tutorials is a vector of numbers. Here it is a Graphs.SimpleGraph, an object that the package knows nothing about. Compared with a vector genotype, three things change:\n\nthe problem says how to draw a random graph (init),\na variation operator says how to make a child graph from a parent (vary), and\nMAPElites is told the genotype type and the variation operator.\n\nThe archive, the emitter and the loop are the same as for vectors.\n\nSet up once, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nRun:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/graphs.jl\n\nusing Graphs: SimpleGraph, add_edge!, add_vertex!, erdos_renyi, global_clustering_coefficient,\n              gdistances, has_edge, ne, nv, rem_edge!, rem_vertex!\nusing QualityDiversity\nusing Random: AbstractRNG, Xoshiro","title":"A custom genotype: graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/graphs/#The-problem","page":"A custom genotype: graphs","text":"We look for graphs that are efficient at carrying traffic, in every combination of how many edges they have and how clustered they are.\n\nThe fitness is the global efficiency: the mean of 1/distance over all pairs of vertices, where a pair that is not connected counts 0. It is 1 for a complete graph.\n\nfunction global_efficiency(g::SimpleGraph)\n    n = nv(g)\n    total = 0.0\n    for source in 1:n, distance in gdistances(g, source)\n        if distance != 0 && distance != typemax(Int)\n            total += 1 / distance\n        end\n    end\n    return total / (n * (n - 1))\nend\nglobal_efficiency (generic function with 1 method)\n\nThe descriptor has two numbers between 0 and 1: the edge density (the fraction of possible edges that are present) and the transitivity (the fraction of connected triples of vertices that close into a triangle).\n\ndensity(g::SimpleGraph) = 2 * ne(g) / (nv(g) * (nv(g) - 1))\nscore(g::SimpleGraph) = (global_efficiency(g), (density(g), global_clustering_coefficient(g)))\nscore (generic function with 1 method)\n\nscore returns (fitness, descriptor), as for a vector genotype. The only new part of the problem is init, which draws a random graph to start from: here a random graph with between 8 and 30 vertices.\n\nrandom_graph(rng::AbstractRNG) = erdos_renyi(rand(rng, 8:30), 0.2 + 0.3 * rand(rng); rng)\n\nprob = QDProblem(score; init=random_graph, bounds=((0, 1), (0, 1)))\nQDProblem{typeof(Main.score), typeof(Main.random_graph), Tuple{Tuple{Float64, Float64}, Tuple{Float64, Float64}}}(Main.score, Main.random_graph, ((0.0, 1.0), (0.0, 1.0)))","title":"The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/graphs/#The-variation-operator","page":"A custom genotype: graphs","text":"A variation operator is a type, a subtype of AbstractVariation, together with a method of QualityDiversity.vary that takes a random number generator, the operator and a parent, and returns a child. The child must be a new graph: the parent stays in the archive and must not change.\n\nOur operator usually flips a random edge (adds it if it is absent, removes it if it is present), and sometimes adds or removes a vertex, within limits on the number of vertices. So the graphs in the archive can have different sizes.\n\nstruct GraphMutation <: AbstractVariation\n    min_nodes::Int\n    max_nodes::Int\nend\n\nfunction QualityDiversity.vary(rng::AbstractRNG, op::GraphMutation, parent::SimpleGraph{Int})\n    child = copy(parent)\n    r = rand(rng)\n    if r < 0.1 && nv(child) < op.max_nodes\n        # a new vertex, joined to a random existing one\n        add_vertex!(child)\n        add_edge!(child, nv(child), rand(rng, 1:nv(child)-1))\n    elseif r < 0.2 && nv(child) > op.min_nodes\n        rem_vertex!(child, rand(rng, 1:nv(child)))\n    else\n        a, b = rand(rng, 1:nv(child)), rand(rng, 1:nv(child))\n        if a != b\n            has_edge(child, a, b) ? rem_edge!(child, a, b) : add_edge!(child, a, b)\n        end\n    end\n    return child\nend","title":"The variation operator"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/graphs/#Running-MAP-Elites","page":"A custom genotype: graphs","text":"MAPElites builds a grid archive for the problem. By default it stores Vector{Float64} genotypes and varies them with a Gaussian mutation; for graphs we pass the genotype type and our operator instead.\n\nalg = MAPElites(prob; dims=(20, 20), batch_size=100, genotype=SimpleGraph{Int},\n                variation=GraphMutation(8, 30))\nresult = solve(prob, alg; budget=100_000, rng=Xoshiro(1))\narchive = result.archive\n\nprintln(length(archive), \" of \", QualityDiversity.capacity(archive), \" cells filled\")\nprintln(\"best efficiency: \", round(max_fitness(archive); digits=3))\n271 of 400 cells filled\nbest efficiency: 1.0\n\nEvery elite is a graph, together with its evaluation:\n\ng, ev = first(QualityDiversity.elites(archive))\n(nv(g), ne(g), ev.fitness, ev.descriptor)\n(12, 23, 0.6666666666666667, [0.3484848484848485, 0.19480519480519481])","title":"Running MAP-Elites"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/graphs/#The-archive","page":"A custom genotype: graphs","text":"The heatmap shows the efficiency of the best graph in every cell; empty cells are left blank. Efficiency rises with density, so compare cells in the same column: at a given density, how much efficiency does clustering cost?\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(size=(560, 460))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"edge density\", ylabel=\"transitivity\", aspect=1)\nhm = heatmap!(ax, archive)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm; label=\"global efficiency\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"The archive"}]
}
